Chapter 1

Introduction

Computer networks enable the connection of devices, exchange of information and access
to online services, but at the same time they create threats for malevolent individuals to
exploit. A network intrusion happens when an unauthorized entity attempts to gain access,
use, interrupt or destroy a network or the devices connected to it. Intrusions can be external,
originating from outside the network, for example, when an intruder scans the network for
open ports or attempts to access the network using weak credentials, or internal, coming
from within the network through a compromised device or user.
Network intrusions can take different forms, varying from the attempts to gain unautho-
rized access like in the case of brute-force attacks, Denial-of-Service attacks that cause
service disruption, interception of traffic through spoofing, transferring data out of the
network after gaining access to a host computer, or attacking other compromised nodes
through command-and-control methods. The variety of intrusion methods requires mul-
tiple approaches to detecting malicious activity since relying on a single method may be
ineffective.
Intrusion Detection and Prevention Systems are used to monitor network traffic and iden-
tify such malicious behaviour. Traditional systems commonly use predefined signatures or
rules to recognize known attacks. These methods can be effective when an attack pattern is
already known, but they may struggle with new attacks or variations that do not match an
existing signature. Machine learning offers another approach by learning patterns in net-
work traffic and identifying suspicious or abnormal behaviour. However, its effectiveness
can depend heavily on the data used to train the model and the environment in which it is
deployed.
Another challenge is that much of modern network communication is encrypted. While
encryption protects user privacy, it also limits the amount of traffic content that can be di-
rectly inspected. This makes network security systems increasingly dependent on other
information, such as traffic patterns, flow behaviour and connection metadata. These chal-
lenges create a need for intrusion detection systems that can combine multiple detection
techniques and remain practical for real network environments.
The aim of this project is to develop a Network Intrusion Detection and Prevention
System (NIDPS) for small networks. The project combines different detection approaches
so that known attacks, abnormal behaviour and suspicious network activity can be identified
and acted upon. The system is designed to operate on a Linux-based device placed directly
in the network path, while also considering practical issues such as encrypted traffic, limited
hardware resources and reliability.
The remainder of this report is organized as follows. Chapter 2 contains the project scope,
goals, and objectives. Chapter 3 reviews the background research and related work on

                                                      1
CHAPTER 1. INTRODUCTION                       2

network intrusion detection and machine learning. Chapter 4 presents the Software Re-
quirements Specification (SRS) of the proposed system. Chapter 5 explains the proposed
approach and methodology used for detection, prevention and evaluation. Chapter 6 de-
scribes the high-level and low-level design of the system.

1.1 Purpose of this Document

The purpose of this document is to present the design, development and evaluation of our
Final Year Project, a Network Intrusion Detection and Prevention System (NIDPS) for
small networks. The project aims to provide a practical security solution that can monitor
network traffic and detect malicious or abnormal activity and respond to identified threats
using a combination of signature-based detection and machine learning.
The primary goal is to assess the protection level provided by this approach while main-
taining its viability for limited hardware. The research also examines how well machine
learning models trained on public intrusion detection datasets perform when tested on dif-
ferent datasets and on real deployment traffic.
The main research questions concern whether a combination of diverse detection techniques
helps in improving the recognition of both normal and abnormal activities, whether the
models are capable of generalizing on different network environments, and whether the
overall system can be operated efficiently without affecting the performance of the normal
network significantly
This report covers the project scope, related research, software requirements, methodology
and system design.

1.2 Intended Audience

This report is mainly directed to the Final Year Project evaluation panel, project advisor,
faculty members and other academic reviewers who will assess the technical quality,
design, implementation and evaluation of the project.
In addition, it may be useful for students, researchers and developers working in the
areas of network security, intrusion detection or machine learning, as it describes the
proposed NIDPS architecture, detection approaches, evaluation methodology and practical
implementation considerations.
The report is written with enough technical detail to explain the system clearly while keep-
ing the discussion understandable for readers with a general background in computer sci-
ence and cyber security.

