1.4 Conclusion

The proposed NIDPS aims to improve the security of small networks by combining multiple
intrusion detection techniques within a single system. The project focuses on the detection
of both known and abnormal network behavior with regard to practical aspects like en-
crypted traffic or the limitations of the available hardware and system reliability. Chapter 1
introduced the background, intention and general direction of the project. The following
chapters elaborate on the related research, software requirements, proposed methodology
and system design.
Chapter 2

Project Vision

This chapter provides an overview of the proposed Network Intrusion Detection and Pre-
vention System. It mainly focuses on describing the purpose of the system, the problem that
the system aims to solve, project objectives, scope, major constraints, identifying stakehold-
ers, and the benefits of the project. Since this project includes both research and develop-
ment, it focuses on building a working NIDPS as well as evaluating how well the selected
intrusion detection techniques perform in different network environments.

2.1 Problem Domain Overview

Our system implements a detection pipeline with three layers to tackle diverse attack vec-
tors. The first layer uses Suricata with the Emerging Threats ruleset to capture known
malicious activity via fixed signatures. The second layer uses an Isolation Forest model to
flag baseline traffic anomalies that lack pre-existing rules. The third layer applies Random
Forest or XGBoost classifiers to categorize flagged events into specific threat types. Instead
of relying on a single indicator, the system aggregates findings across all three layers before
taking action, either allowing the connection, logging an alert, or initiating a block.
To protect user privacy and minimize processing overhead, the system avoids decrypting
packet payloads. Instead, it evaluates connection behavior through flow statistics and TLS
parameters, specifically JA3 and JA3S fingerprinting. When malicious activity is con-
firmed, an automated response module updates local firewall configurations using iptables
and ipset to block the origin host immediately. A web-based management interface pro-
vides administrators with system telemetry, security logs, and a list of currently blocked
endpoints.
The research component focuses on model adaptability across changing network condi-
tions. Machine learning models often suffer performance degradation when deployed out-
side their initial training data. To evaluate cross-environment resilience, we test our models
on benchmark datasets as well as live traffic captured directly from our access point deploy-
ment.

2.2 Problem Statement

Existing intrusion detection approaches have their limitations. Signature based systems are
effective against known attacks, but they may miss new attacks or modified versions of ex-
isting ones. Machine learning approaches can identify unusual traffic, but their performance

                                                      5
CHAPTER 2. PROJECT VISION  6

may drop when they are used on a network that is different from the dataset on which they
were trained.
The problem addressed by this project is therefore the lack of an affordable and practical
solution for small networks that can combine known-attack detection, anomaly detection,
and attack classification, while still run efficiently on limited hardware. The system must
also be able to deal with encrypted traffic, avoid excessive false positives, and continue
behaving safely if one of its components fails.

2.3 Problem Elaboration

The overall problem can be divided into smaller problems that need to be handled together.
First, no single detection method works for everything. Signature tools like Suricata catch
known attacks instantly, but they miss brand new threats. On the flip side, machine learn-
ing spots weird behavior, but it can trigger false alarms on normal traffic. Using just one
approach leaves major security gaps, so the system needs a multi-layer setup.
Second, machine learning models often struggle when moved to a new environment. A
model might get perfect scores on one dataset, but completely fail on a different network
with different devices and user habits. To test real-world reliability, we evaluate our models
across multiple datasets, like CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018, and CIC-IoT-2023, as
well as live traffic from our own access point.
Third, most internet traffic is encrypted. Trying to decode network traffic is too taxing on
the hardware and invasive to user privacy. Instead, the system reads connection behaviors,
traffic patterns, and TLS handshake plaintexts (JA3 and JA3S).
Fourth, automatic blocking can backfire if the system makes a mistake. While blocking
bad actors via iptables and ipset stops attacks fast, false alarms can accidentally lock out real
users. To prevent this, the response engine includes safeguards like allowlists, temporary
blocks, and adjustable threat thresholds.
Finally, the system runs on low power hardware. If the NIDPS uses too much CPU or
memory, it will slow down internet speeds for everyone on the network. To keep things fast
and stable, we use lightweight ML models, process data off-path, and set up fail-safes so a
system crash doesn’t knock out the entire internet connection.

2.4 Goals and Objectives

The main goal of this project is to build and evaluate a practical hybrid NIDPS that protects
small networks using both signature matching and machine learning.
The main objectives include:

    • Hardware & Deployment: Implement an inline NIDPS between client devices and
       the ISP gateway, complete with a watchdog and auto-recovery rules to prevent net-
       work downtime.
CHAPTER 2. PROJECT VISION  7

    • Multi-Layer Detection: Combine Suricata (using Emerging Threats rules) for
       known attacks, Isolation Forest for unseen traffic anomalies, and Random For-
       est/XGBoost for classifying specific threats.

    • Encrypted Traffic Analysis: Assess encrypted connections using flow statistics and
       TLS fingerprints (JA3/JA3S) without decrypting user payloads.

    • Automated Mitigation: Automatically block confirmed threats using Linux firewall
       rules (iptables/ipset).

    • Cross-Environment Research: Train and test models across CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-
       CIC-IDS2018, and CIC-IoT-2023, and benchmark their performance on live traffic
       from our actual access point.

    • Management & Telemetry: Build a web dashboard for live monitoring, alerts, and
       system controls, measuring throughput, latency, CPU/memory usage, and detection
       accuracy under real traffic.

2.5 Project Scope

The system will operate directly as a wireless access point positioned between connected
devices and the ISP gateway. Core features include:

    • Multi-Layer Detection: Combines Suricata to catch known attack rules, Isolation
       Forest to spot unusual traffic spikes, and Random Forest or XGBoost to figure out the
       exact attack type.

    • Encrypted Traffic Analysis: Looks at connection patterns and unencrypted TLS
       signatures to check if a stream is safe, completely skipping payload decryption to
       keep user privacy intact.

    • Active Response: Proactively take actions such as dropping the offending IP from a
       network by triggering automated responses while providing a intuitive web interface
       to triage and manage alerts and blocked devices.

    • Research and Testing: Evaluate the accuracy of the framework against several
       benchmark datasets and network traffic captures from our infrastructure while main-
       taining memory and CPU footprint under reasonable limits for small scale hardware.

Key Deliverables

    • A working NIDPS access point prototype with automated prevention and fail-safe
       controls

    • The complete ML model training, testing, and cross-dataset evaluation pipeline

    • A web management dashboard

    • Practical performance benchmarks (latency, resource usage, detection accuracy)
CHAPTER 2. PROJECT VISION  8

    • Full system design and research documentation.

Out of Scope

To maintain a clear focus, the project will not:
    • Function as an enterprise-grade security suite or support enterprise-scale hosting.
    • Monitor traffic from devices bypassing the access point.
    • Decrypt user traffic payloads.
    • Cover every existing exploit category or run continuous online model retraining on
       the live device.

2.6 Sustainable Development Goal (SDG)

Figure 2.1: The figures display the orange SDG 9 icon (Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure) and
blue SDG 16 icon (Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions), representing resilient tech frameworks
and safe digital environments.

Our project mainly focuses on SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure. Mod-
ern digital services rely heavily on secure and stable network connections. By adapting
advanced threat detection for low-cost, resource-constrained hardware, this project offers
an accessible way to make small scale networks more resilient against cyberattacks.
It also contributes to SDG 16: Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions by supporting safe
online spaces where users are protected against unauthorized access, data disclosure, and
attacks on their network.

2.7 Constraints

Building and running this system comes with a few limitations:

    • Hardware Constraints: Low-cost hardware is unable to withstand the demands of
       resource-heavy software. Memory consumption, as well as processor utilization,
       must be kept at a minimum, which rules out the use of heavy ML models.
CHAPTER 2. PROJECT VISION  9

• Network Latency: Being in the middle of the track means that any additional delay
  introduced by the system will add up to the overall latency of the Internet. Traffic
  processing must be fast to keep connections smooth.

• Encrypted Traffic: Since packet contents are hidden, reading payloads is off the
  table. The ML side has to rely only on connection patterns and TLS details.

• Data Limits: Public security datasets do not cover every real-world scenario. That
  is why testing across multiple datasets and live traffic is necessary.

• Coverage Gaps: The system can only monitor devices that connect directly through
  its access point. Any device using a different network stays invisible.

• Blocking Mistakes: Too aggressive blocking prevents legitimate users, while too
  lenient allows threats through, requires careful adjustment and default safety settings.

2.8 Business Opportunity

Most small offices, research labs, and home users cannot afford costly corporate security
tools. Moreover, they rarely have a competent IT staff that would be able to constantly
monitor and adjust complicated security software. This makes an affordable Linux-based
solution that uses standard security hardware attractive. It makes all devices connected to
it secure without any additional software. By only implementing the traffic control at the
regional access point level, it makes all the devices connected to it safe and secure without
any additional hassle. While this project is centered on research and a working prototype,
it builds a solid base for a practical product aimed at smaller networks.

2.9 Stakeholders Description/ User Characteristics

Network Owners and Admins: The primary users of the system which involve configura-
tion of alerts, blocked devices, and system preferences. Since these are not security experts,
the system is designed to provide informative feedback and employ safety defaults.
Regular Network Users: These are the individuals and devices which access the WI-FI
network. They are not direct users of the dashboard, but benefit from its existence, as it
helps to maintain and optimize the network.
The Project Team: This team is responsible for the development of the system, including
the coding, training, and testing of algorithms.
Advisors and Evaluators: Faculty members who review the details of the research, eval-
uate the code, and ensure that the project’s objectives are met.
Future Researchers: Potential students and developers who will review the project’s de-
tails and possibly adopt it in their own research.
CHAPTER 2. PROJECT VISION  10

2.10 Conclusion

The contents of chapter 2 provided an overview of the proposed NIDPS project, which
included the problem statement, major aspects and issues, and objectives of the intended
solution. In this chapter, we have discussed the project scope, key objectives, constraints,
stakeholders, and potential benefits for business, and specified the relevant Sustainable De-
velopment Goal. Thus, this chapter sets the course and boundaries of the project, providing
the basis for the requirements, methods, design, and implementation that will be elaborated
in subsequent chapters.
Chapter 3

Literature Review / Related Work

This chapter reviews recent research related to Network Intrusion Detection and Prevention
Systems (NIDPS). It focuses on the detection techniques, datasets, machine learning mod-
els, deployment approaches and evaluation methods used in previous studies. Each study
is summarised, critically analysed and related to the proposed system, and the chapter ends
with summary tables of the reviewed literature, the datasets they use and the systems closest
to the proposed NIDPS.

3.1 Definitions, Acronyms, and Abbreviations

The important definitions, acronyms and abbreviations used in this chapter are listed below:
    • AUC: Area Under the (ROC) Curve
    • CIC: Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity
    • DT: Decision Tree
    • EIF: Extended Isolation Forest
    • F1-score: Harmonic mean of precision and recall
    • FPR: False Positive Rate
    • IDS: Intrusion Detection System
    • IIoT: Industrial Internet of Things
    • IoT: Internet of Things
    • iForest / IF: Isolation Forest
    • IPS: Intrusion Prevention System
    • LSTM: Long Short-Term Memory (recurrent neural network)
    • ML: Machine Learning
    • NIDPS: Network Intrusion Detection and Prevention System
    • NIDS: Network Intrusion Detection System
    • OC-SVM: One-Class Support Vector Machine
    • RF: Random Forest
    • SVM: Support Vector Machine

                                                     11
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK                                 12

• XGBoost: Extreme Gradient Boosting
• Zeek: Open-source network security monitor that produces connection logs

3.2 Detailed Literature Review

This section covers notable literature on hybrid signature and machine learning intrusion
detection, unsupervised anomaly detection with Isolation Forest, supervised attack classifi-
cation with tree ensembles, and the deployment and generalisation of ML-based detectors.
Each item is presented with a summary, a critical analysis and its relationship to the pro-
posed NIDPS.

3.2.1 Performance Comparison of Intrusion Detection Systems and
        Application of Machine Learning to Snort

Snort and Suricata are compared on a 10 Gbps network, and machine learning is added to
Snort to reduce its false alarms. An SVM optimised with the firefly algorithm lowers the
false positive rate to 8.6%.

3.2.1.1 Summary of the Research Item

Shah and Issac [1] compared the two most widely used open-source signature engines,
Snort and Suricata, running on identical machines at a traffic rate of 10 Gbps. Suricata
handled the high traffic speed with a lower packet drop rate because of its multi-threaded
design, but used more computing resources, while Snort showed slightly higher detection
accuracy. Since Snort produced a large number of false positive alarms, the authors de-
veloped an adaptive Snort plug-in and compared several learning algorithms to filter its
alerts. SVM was selected, and the best configuration, an SVM optimised with the firefly
algorithm, achieved a false positive rate of 8.6% and a false negative rate of 2.2%.

3.2.1.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It gives a practical, measured comparison of Snort and Suricata under heavy load,
       which is useful when choosing the signature engine for an inline system.

    • It shows clearly that machine learning can reduce the false alarms of a signature
       engine.

    • The SVM models were evaluated offline; SVMs are relatively heavy, and the work
       does not show the approach running on live traffic on low-cost hardware.

    • The system only detects and does not block traffic, and it does not consider encrypted
       traffic.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  13

3.2.1.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This work supports the use of Suricata as the first layer of the proposed NIDPS because
it copes better with high traffic rates, and it supports the idea of using machine learning
to reduce the false positives of a signature engine. Unlike this work, the proposed system
uses lightweight tree models that classify flows in milliseconds and acts on the verdict by
blocking the offending host.

3.2.2 A Novel Hybrid Intrusion Detection Method Integrating
        Anomaly Detection with Misuse Detection

A C4.5 decision tree (misuse detection) splits the normal training data into subsets, and
a separate one-class SVM is trained on each subset for anomaly detection. Evaluated on
NSL-KDD.

3.2.2.1 Summary of the Research Item

Kim, Lee and Kim [2] proposed a hierarchical hybrid intrusion detection method in which
a misuse detection model and an anomaly detection model are combined in a decomposed
structure. A C4.5 decision tree is first trained as the misuse detector, and it is also used
to divide the normal training data into smaller, more uniform subsets. A one-class SVM
is then trained on each subset, so every anomaly model describes one region of normal
behaviour more precisely and indirectly benefits from the knowledge of known attacks. On
the NSL-KDD dataset the method detected both known and unknown attacks better than
conventional methods while keeping false positives low, and the anomaly model needed
only about 50% of the training time and 60% of the testing time of the conventional models.

3.2.2.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • The decomposition of normal traffic gives more accurate anomaly models and no-
       ticeably lower training and testing time.

    • It shows that relying on a single model to do all the work produces more false posi-
       tives than letting each component handle the traffic it is best at.

    • The evaluation uses only NSL-KDD, a dataset derived from 1998 traffic that does not
       represent modern or encrypted traffic.

    • It is an offline detection method; no prevention or deployment on a real network is
       shown.

3.2.2.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The proposed NIDPS follows the same principle of combining misuse (signature) detection
with anomaly detection so that each part handles the traffic it suits best. It replaces the
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  14

decision tree with Suricata rules and a Random Forest/XGBoost classifier, uses Isolation
Forest instead of one-class SVM for cheaper anomaly scoring, and evaluates on modern
datasets and live traffic.

3.2.3 Collaborative and Hybrid Network Intrusion Detection
        Framework Based on Suricata and Isolation Forest

Suricata detects known attacks and an Isolation Forest flags anomalies that may be unknown
attacks. Several NIDS instances share alerts through a central log.

3.2.3.1 Summary of the Research Item

Chiba et al. [3] proposed a hybrid and collaborative NIDS framework. Suricata performs
signature-based detection of known attacks and was chosen over Snort because it is multi-
threaded and divides its workload across processing resources. Traffic that does not match
any signature is examined by an Isolation Forest, which detects anomalies that may indicate
unknown attacks. Since Isolation Forest does not depend on distance or density calcula-
tions, the authors argue that it keeps the computational cost low. The NIDS instances are
collaborative: alerts are stored in a central log so that an attack detected by one sensor is
also recognised by the others.

3.2.3.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • The pairing of Suricata with Isolation Forest is lightweight and matches the needs of
       edge devices.

    • Sharing confirmed attacks between sensors lets the whole network benefit from one
       detection.

    • It is mainly a design proposal: there is no attack-classification stage and only limited
       experimental evaluation.

    • It does not describe automated blocking, failure handling or the analysis of encrypted
       traffic.

3.2.3.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This is one of the works closest to the proposed NIDPS, which also places Suricata first
and sends traffic to an Isolation Forest. The proposed system extends this idea into a com-
plete three-layer implementation by adding a supervised attack classifier, decision fusion,
automated blocking and a watchdog, all running on low-cost hardware for home and small-
office networks.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  15

3.2.4 Self-Healing Hybrid Intrusion Detection System: An Ensemble
        Machine Learning Approach

A C5 decision tree acts as the signature stage, an LSTM detects anomalies, and features of
detected anomalies are turned into new signatures that are fed back into the first stage.

3.2.4.1 Summary of the Research Item

Kushal et al. [4] proposed a three-stage self-healing hybrid IDS. In the first stage, a C5
decision tree trained on UNSW-NB15 classifies packets as normal or attack and acts as the
signature-based detector. Packets that it does not match are passed to the second stage, an
LSTM recurrent neural network trained on ADFA-LD, which flags deviations from normal
behaviour. In the third stage, the attributes of anomalous packets are extracted and added
to the training data of the C5 stage, so the system learns new signatures and can detect
similar attacks earlier. The C5 classifier reached a true positive rate of about 97% with a
false positive rate of about 8%, while the LSTM reached a detection rate of about 90% with
a false alarm rate of 17% and an AUC of 0.936.

3.2.4.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • Turning detected anomalies into new signatures is an interesting way to keep the
       detector up to date without manual work.

    • The two stages were evaluated separately on different datasets, and the authors note
       that the performance of the integrated system still needs to be assessed.

    • The deep learning stage is computationally expensive and has a high false alarm rate,
       so wrongly learned signatures could classify dangerous traffic as normal or block
       benign traffic.

    • Real-time testing on live networks is left as future work.

3.2.4.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The proposed NIDPS shares the goal of adapting to the local network, but keeps a fixed,
verified set of models on the device and adapts only the anomaly threshold through calibra-
tion on normal traffic. This keeps the system adaptable while avoiding the cost and risk of
learning new signatures online.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  16

3.2.5 Comparative Evaluation of Machine Learning and
        Signature-Based NIDS for Multi-Class and Binary Threat
        Detection

Snort is compared with Random Forest, XGBoost and Decision Tree on UNSW-NB15. The
ensemble models clearly outperform Snort in accuracy and false positive rate.

3.2.5.1 Summary of the Research Item

Wen-Udeoji, Muyeba and Mohammadi [5] built an empirical framework to compare the
signature-based Snort NIDS with three machine learning models, Random Forest, XGBoost
and Decision Tree, for both binary and multi-class threat detection on the UNSW-NB15
dataset while keeping its real class distribution. Random Forest reached 87% accuracy
compared with 56.21% for Snort, with a false positive rate of 25% against 62.5% for Snort.
Decision Tree and XGBoost reached 80.2% and 82.84% accuracy respectively. The au-
thors conclude that the results support hybrid strategies that combine signature-based and
machine learning detection.

3.2.5.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It is a recent, direct comparison of a signature engine with tree-based ML models on
       the same data.

    • Keeping the natural class distribution gives a more realistic picture than artificially
       balanced data.

    • Only one dataset is used, and the hybrid approach that the authors recommend is not
       implemented or evaluated.

    • Real-time performance and resource use are not reported.

3.2.5.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The results support the choice of tree ensembles (Random Forest and XGBoost) for Layer
3 of the proposed NIDPS and the decision to keep the signature engine and the ML models
as separate layers whose outputs are fused, rather than relying on either one alone.

3.2.6 Real-Time Hybrid Signature and Machine Learning Intrusion
        Detection

A signature engine is combined with deep learning models for real-time detection, and
the decisions of both detection stages are combined after each stage has produced its own
output.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  17

3.2.6.1 Summary of the Research Item

Naik et al. [6] presented a real-time hybrid intrusion detection approach in which a
signature-based engine detects known attacks and deep learning models detect anomalous
behaviour. Each detection stage produces its own decision, and their experiments showed
that combining these decisions at the end (late fusion) gives better results than passing the
output of one stage as input to the other.

3.2.6.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It shows the benefit of late fusion of independent detector outputs in a real-time set-
       ting.

    • Deep learning models need more processing power, which makes the approach harder
       to run inline on low-cost hardware.

    • Automated prevention, encrypted traffic and cross-dataset evaluation are not ad-
       dressed.

3.2.6.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The proposed NIDPS also uses late fusion across its three layers, but with optimised tree
models instead of deep learning. Each layer’s output is recorded, so the system can show
which layer made the decision and why before deciding whether to pass, alert or block.

3.2.7 Improving Detection Accuracy of Network Intrusions Using a
        Hybrid NIDS Based on Isolation Forest and Random Forest

Suricata is combined with an Isolation Forest that flags anomalous traffic and a Random
Forest that identifies the attack type. It reaches 96.33% accuracy on CIC-IDS-2017.

3.2.7.1 Summary of the Research Item

Wang and Avrianto [7] proposed a hybrid NIDS in which Suricata performs real-time packet
inspection, an Isolation Forest flags anomalous traffic and a Random Forest identifies the
family of the attack. The models were trained on pre-processed captured traffic and eval-
uated on CIC-IDS-2017 and on simulated attack data. The hybrid model reached 96.33%
accuracy on CIC-IDS-2017 and 99.86% on the simulated data, and compared with stan-
dalone Suricata it detected more unknown threats with fewer false positives.

3.2.7.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  18

    • It is the closest published design to the proposed system and shows that Isolation
       Forest and Random Forest work well together.

    • The evaluation on CIC-IDS-2017 covers a limited subset of attacks, mainly DoS and
       port scanning.

    • The very high accuracy is reported on simulated data, which may not reflect real
       network traffic.

    • The system detects but does not block traffic, and failure handling is not considered.

3.2.7.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This is the most similar approach to the proposed NIDPS. The proposed system keeps the
Isolation Forest and Random Forest pairing, but places Suricata inline in IPS mode as the
first layer, adds automated blocking and failure handling, and is evaluated on three public
datasets together with a self-collected dataset.

3.2.8 Extended Isolation Forest for Intrusion Detection in Zeek Data

The hyperparameters of the Extended Isolation Forest are tuned to detect malicious con-
nections in Zeek logs. The simplest configuration (extension level 0) performs best.

3.2.8.1 Summary of the Research Item

Moomtaheen et al. [8] tuned the hyperparameters of the Extended Isolation Forest (EIF), a
variant of Isolation Forest, to detect malicious traffic in Zeek connection logs. The authors
created a new dataset, UWF-ZeekDataFall22, and used Hadoop for storage and Apache
Spark for training. The model reached accuracies of 82.3% for the Resource Development
tactic, 82.21% for Reconnaissance and 78.3% for Discovery. The best results came from
extension level 0, which is equivalent to the standard Isolation Forest.

3.2.8.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It uses real Zeek connection logs labelled with MITRE ATT&CK tactics, which is
       closer to real deployments than older benchmarks.

    • It shows that the extended variant gives no benefit over the basic Isolation Forest on
       this data.

    • Accuracy around 80% is moderate, so Isolation Forest alone is not enough for reliable
       detection.

    • The big-data pipeline (Hadoop and Spark) is not suited to small, resource-constrained
       devices.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  19

3.2.8.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This study supports the decision to use the standard Isolation Forest in Layer 2 of the
proposed NIDPS rather than a more complex variant, and to combine it with a signature
layer and a supervised classifier because its accuracy on its own is moderate.

3.2.9 An Optimized Isolation Forest Based Intrusion Detection
        System for Heterogeneous and Streaming Data in IIoT
        Networks

An Isolation Forest tuned with a modified Harris Hawks Optimisation (ERHHO) reduces
features and training time and handles streaming data with concept drift.

3.2.9.1 Summary of the Research Item

Elsaid and Binbusayyis [9] proposed OIFIDS, an Isolation Forest based intrusion detection
system for Industrial IoT networks. The Isolation Forest is optimised with ERHHO, a
modified Harris Hawks Optimisation algorithm, which reduces the number of features and
the training time. The system was evaluated on NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15 and CSE-CIC-
IDS2018, reaching accuracies of 95.6%, 94.8% and 99% respectively. On streaming data
with concept drift it achieved AUC values of 0.948, 0.922 and 0.97 on the three datasets.

3.2.9.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It addresses streaming traffic and concept drift, which are real problems for a de-
       ployed detector.

    • Feature reduction lowers computational complexity, which suits resource-constrained
       devices.

    • The optimisation step adds complexity to training, and the results are still on public
       benchmarks rather than a live deployment.

3.2.9.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The proposed NIDPS also has to cope with traffic that changes over time. Instead of a
metaheuristic optimiser, it handles this with feature selection and a periodic recalibration
of the Isolation Forest threshold on the local network’s normal traffic.

3.2.10 Improved Isolation Forest with Sparse Auto-Encoder Feature
          Extraction

A sparse auto-encoder extracts features and a new split-point rule improves the Isolation
Forest, giving better accuracy, recall, F1-score and AUC on KDDCUP99.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  20

3.2.10.1 Summary of the Research Item

Yu et al. [10] improved the Isolation Forest for intrusion detection in two ways. First, a
sparse auto-encoder is used to extract better features from the raw traffic records. Second,
a new rule is used to choose the split points of the isolation trees. Evaluated on the KDD-
CUP99 dataset, the improved model achieved higher accuracy, recall, F1-score and AUC
than the standard Isolation Forest.

3.2.10.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It shows that feature quality has a large effect on how well Isolation Forest separates
       attacks.

    • KDDCUP99 is outdated and contains many redundant records, so the results may not
       hold on modern traffic.

    • The auto-encoder adds training and inference cost compared with the plain Isolation
       Forest.

3.2.10.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This work confirms that the proposed NIDPS should invest in good flow features for
its Isolation Forest. The proposed system obtains them from a consistent flow extractor
and feature selection rather than from a neural auto-encoder, keeping the anomaly layer
lightweight.

3.2.11 An Improved X-Means and Isolation Forest Based
          Methodology for Network Traffic Anomaly Detection

X-iForest removes the need to know the anomaly ratio in advance by clustering the sus-
pected anomalies. It improves average AUC by 8.1% over the basic Isolation Forest.

3.2.11.1 Summary of the Research Item

Feng et al. [11] addressed a common problem of Isolation Forest: the contamination pa-
rameter (the share of anomalies) must be guessed, although the true ratio is unknown in
real traffic. Their method, X-iForest, first runs an Isolation Forest with a deliberately high
contamination value, then measures the distance of each suspected anomaly from the centre
of the normal data, removes extreme values and uses X-means and 2-means clustering to
separate real anomalies from normal points. Across four simulated and four public datasets,
the average AUC was 8.1% higher than the basic Isolation Forest, and the method had the
best AUC on six of the eight datasets.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  21

3.2.11.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It removes the need to guess the contamination parameter, which is a real practical
       issue.

    • The evaluation datasets are general anomaly-detection datasets and simulated Gaus-
       sian data, not network intrusion datasets.

    • Performance is weaker on small datasets, and the method has not been tested on live
       traffic.

3.2.11.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

The proposed NIDPS faces the same problem of setting the anomaly threshold without
knowing the true anomaly ratio. It solves it by calibrating the Isolation Forest threshold on
a period of normal traffic from the local network, which is simpler to run on the device.

3.2.12 Intrusion Detection: A Comparison Study of Machine
          Learning Models Using Unbalanced Dataset

Five classifiers are compared on the imbalanced UNSW-NB15 dataset. Random Forest and
XGBoost give the best overall results.

3.2.12.1 Summary of the Research Item

Ajagbe, Awotunde and Florez [12] compared five machine learning models, Linear SVC,
Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Decision Tree and XGBoost, on the UNSW-NB15
dataset, in which about 92.7% of the records are normal and 7.3% are attacks. Random
Forest and XGBoost performed best in accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score. The AUC-
ROC was 0.91 for Random Forest and Linear SVC, 0.90 for XGBoost, 0.83 for Decision
Tree and 0.70 for Logistic Regression.

3.2.12.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It evaluates the models on imbalanced data, which reflects real networks where at-
       tacks are rare.

    • The way oversampling and undersampling were applied is not clearly described.

    • The study was not tested in a real-world setting and uses a single dataset.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  22

3.2.12.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work
This comparison supports the choice of Random Forest and XGBoost for the attack classi-
fier (Layer 3) of the proposed NIDPS, and highlights class imbalance as an issue that the
training pipeline must handle.

3.2.13 Enhanced Intrusion Detection Systems Performance with
         UNSW-NB15 Data Analysis

Four supervised classifiers are compared after correlation-based feature selection. Random
Forest performs best with an F1-score of 97.80%.

3.2.13.1 Summary of the Research Item
More et al. [13] compared Logistic Regression, SVM, Decision Tree and Random Forest
on UNSW-NB15 after exploratory data analysis, correlation-based feature selection and
random sampling. Random Forest was the best model, with an F1-score of 97.80%, an
accuracy of 98.63% and a false alarm rate of 1.36%.

3.2.13.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item
The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It shows strong Random Forest results with a low false alarm rate after simple feature
       selection.

    • Results come from a single dataset, so they say little about generalisation to other
       networks.

    • Detection latency and resource use are not reported.

3.2.13.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work
The study supports the use of Random Forest in Layer 3 of the proposed NIDPS. The
proposed system goes further by also measuring latency and resource use and by testing the
model across datasets.

3.2.14 An Optimized Gradient Boosting Framework for IoT
          Intrusion Detection on the CICIoT2023 Dataset

XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost are evaluated on CICIoT2023 with a focus on edge
deployment. Reducing 46 features to 23 cuts inference latency by 30–51% with almost no
loss of accuracy.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  23

3.2.14.1 Summary of the Research Item

Almahaqeri et al. [14] proposed an optimised gradient boosting intrusion detection frame-
work for IoT environments, with an emphasis on practical deployment at the edge. An
intrusion detection system on an edge gateway must not only detect attacks accurately but
also run efficiently with limited computing power. The framework was evaluated on the CI-
CIoT2023 dataset, comparing XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost for binary, attack-family
(8-class) and fine-grained (34-class) classification.
Training is performed offline, while the deployed edge system performs online flow clas-
sification. To reduce the processing cost, the authors apply gain-based feature selection
and reduce the 46 features to 23, which lowered inference latency by around 30–51% and
training time by 33–40% with almost no loss in predictive performance. XGBoost achieved
99.61% accuracy and a macro F1-score of 0.995 in binary detection, taking 0.355 microsec-
onds per sample.

3.2.14.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It takes into account both the deployment efficiency and detection accuracy, which
       many intrusion detection works failed to consider.

    • It demonstrates that reducing the feature set significantly increases the inference
       speed without hurting the accuracy, which is particularly suitable for edge computing
       scenarios.

    • The evaluation was restricted to the CICIoT2023, therefore, the extent to which
       the method can be generalized to other network types has yet to be demonstrated,
       however, the authors have pointed out that cross-validation on different datasets and
       streaming conditions are possible future work.

    • Edge-suitability was evaluated in terms of latency rather than actual deployment on
       a low-power gateway and only the flow-level features are used.

3.2.14.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This work is closely related to the proposed NIDPS, which also performs detection on a
resource-constrained Linux gateway. Like the paper, the proposed system separates offline
training from online inference and treats latency and resource use as evaluation criteria.
Unlike this study, it also includes signature-based and anomaly detection, cross-dataset
evaluation, automated blocking, failure handling and testing on traffic from the actual de-
ployment.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK                             24

3.2.15 Cross-Dataset Generalisation of Deep Learning Intrusion
         Detection Models

Deep IDS models that score about 99% on the dataset they were trained on drop to roughly
50% when tested on a different dataset.

3.2.15.1 Summary of the Research Item

Xin and Xu [15] examined how well intrusion detection models generalise beyond the
dataset they were trained on. They report that deep IDS models which reach around 99%
on their own dataset often fall to about 50%, close to random guessing, when evaluated on
other datasets. This shows that high benchmark scores do not guarantee good performance
on a different network.

3.2.15.2 Critical Analysis of the Research Item

The strengths and weaknesses of this work are:

    • It exposes a major weakness of single-dataset evaluation, which most reviewed stud-
       ies rely on.

    • It focuses on detection accuracy and does not propose a deployable system or measure
       runtime cost.

3.2.15.3 Relationship to the Proposed Research Work

This finding is the main reason why the proposed NIDPS is trained on CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-
CIC-IDS2018 and CIC-IoT-2023 with a common flow extractor, evaluated across datasets,
and validated on traffic collected from the deployment network itself.

