The existing systems mainly address one or two of these issues, whereas the proposed
system improves on them in five ways:

    • Complete system: Previous works either propose a design or only detect traffic. The
       proposed system detects, blocks and applies safeguards, while keeping forwarding
       latency close to that of a regular router.

    • Three layers with fusion: Suricata catches known attacks first, an Isolation Forest
       tuned to local traffic spots anomalous behaviour, and a Random Forest/XGBoost clas-
       sifier identifies the attack family. Their outputs are fused so that the system records
       what each layer decided and why.

    • Lightweight models: Unlike many deep learning systems, the proposed system uses
       lightweight tree models that run beside the forwarding path rather than in it. They
       check each flow once it is complete, or every few seconds for long-running flows,
       so they add no delay to forwarded traffic. Latency, throughput and resource use are
       measured.
CHAPTER 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / RELATED WORK  28

    • Cross-dataset evaluation: Because most models fail on unseen datasets, the system
       is trained on CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018 and CIC-IoT-2023 using one flow
       extractor and validated on self-collected household traffic.

    • Encrypted traffic and failure handling: The system analyses encrypted connec-
       tions using JA3/JA3S fingerprints and flow metadata without decryption, and uses a
       watchdog with configurable fail-open, fail-closed and degraded modes. None of the
       reviewed systems addresses either problem.

The contribution of the proposed project is therefore not the idea of combining signature
detection with machine learning, which has been researched extensively, but turning that
idea into a practical, deployable system for home and small-office networks.

3.6 Conclusion

The reviewed literature shows a clear direction: signature engines such as Snort and Suri-
cata detect known attacks reliably but miss new ones and raise many false alarms, while
machine learning detects unknown behaviour but is rarely deployed inline. Hybrid systems
that let each technique handle the traffic it suits best, and that fuse their decisions at the
end, give the best results [2, 5, 6]. Isolation Forest is a cheap and effective anomaly detec-
tor when it is given good features and a sensible threshold [8, 10, 11], and tree ensembles
such as Random Forest and XGBoost are the strongest and fastest choice for classifying
known attack types [12, 13, 14].
The review also exposed common gaps. Most studies use a single, often outdated dataset,
and models that score close to 99% on one dataset can fall to about 50% on another [15].
Few works report latency or resource use, few implement automated blocking, and none of
the reviewed systems analyses encrypted traffic or defines what happens when a component
fails. The proposed NIDPS is designed around these gaps: it combines Suricata, a calibrated
Isolation Forest and a Random Forest/XGBoost classifier with late fusion, blocks confirmed
attackers automatically, analyses encrypted traffic through flow metadata and JA3/JA3S
fingerprints, is evaluated across three public datasets and real traffic, and runs on low-cost
hardware with defined failure modes.
Chapter 4

Software Requirement Specifications

This part will explain the requirements of the proposed Network Intrusion Detection and
Prevention System. It will go through the workings of the system, what its required bench-
marks are and the conditions in which it can be used. The level of detail will be such that
it can be properly built and tested. We start with a feature list and functional requirements
which have been categorized. Further on we discuss quality attributes and non-functional
requirements followed by assumptions regarding the system. Interactions between different
users and the system are modeled as use cases. The chapter closes with the hardware and
software requirements, the design of the graphical user interface, the database design and
an analysis of project risks. Requirements are given identifiers (for example FR-2.1) so that
they can be traced to the design in Chapter 6 and to the tests in Section 6.6.6.
The system has five classes of users or actors, which are referred to throughout this chapter:

    • Network Administrator: the owner of the home or small-office network who installs
       the NIDPS and manages it through the web dashboard. This user is not assumed to
       be a security expert.

    • Network User (client device): any phone, laptop or IoT device that connects to the
       NIDPS access point. These users do not interact with the NIDPS directly; they are
       protected by it.

    • Attacker: an external host or a compromised device inside the network that generates
       malicious traffic. The attacker triggers detection and prevention use cases.

    • System Scheduler / Watchdog: internal time- and event-driven actors that run
       scheduled jobs (rule updates, threshold calibration, data purging) and supervise the
       health of every component.

    • Model Maintainer: a project team member who trains and validates machine learn-
       ing models offline and publishes model packages to the device.

