4.2 Functional Requirements

The functional requirements below describe what the system must do. They are grouped by
the features listed in Section 4.1, and each requirement is numbered according to its feature.

4.2.1 NIDPS-1: Inline Wireless Access Point

FR-1.1 The system shall act as a WAP, and its network name will be configurable along
             with being password protected.

FR-1.2 The system shall assign IP addresses to any devices that are connected using
             DHCP.

FR-1.3 The system shall be acting as the DNS server for any connected devices.
FR-1.4 The system shall pass client traffic on to the ISP router using NAT over its wired

             connection.

FR-1.5 The system shall stop wireless clients from talking to each other directly, so that
             traffic between them also passes through the NIDPS.

4.2.2 NIDPS-2: Signature-Based Detection (Layer 1)

FR-2.1 The system shall send all forwarded traffic to Suricata so it can be checked before
             it is passed on.

FR-2.2 The system shall check every packet against the Emerging Threats Open ruleset.
FR-2.3 The system shall drop packets that match rules set to "drop" and allow all other

             packets through.

FR-2.4 The system shall use custom rules to detect SSH brute force, port scans, DNS
             spoofing, SYN floods and known malicious TLS fingerprints.

FR-2.5 The system shall detect ARP spoofing by comparing ARP replies with the de-
             vices it has assigned addresses to.

FR-2.6 The system shall pass every alert, with its source, destination, rule and severity,
             on to the other layers.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                                        32

4.2.3 NIDPS-3: TLS Fingerprints and Flow Extraction

FR-3.1 The system shall group packets into flows, where a flow is a connec-
             tion/conversation between devices.

FR-3.2 The system shall end a flow when it is closed, when inactive for a set amount of
             time, or when it has been active for a set amount of time.

FR-3.3 The system shall extract 78 features for flows from the packets that arrive.

FR-3.4 The system shall read identifying details (JA3, JA3S and SNI) from encrypted
             TLS connections and attach them to the flow, without decrypting the traffic.

4.2.4 NIDPS-4: Anomaly Detection (Layer 2)

FR-4.1 The system shall check that each flow matches the features expected by the
             current model and reject any that do not.

FR-4.2 The system shall give each flow an anomaly score between 0 and 1 using an
             Isolation Forest.

FR-4.3 The system shall mark a flow as anomalous when its score is above the anomaly
             threshold.

FR-4.4 The system shall set the anomaly threshold based on normal traffic from the
             local network, leaving out traffic linked to alerts or blocked devices.

FR-4.5 The system shall learn how much data each device normally sends out and flag
             it when a device sends far more than usual (possible data theft).

FR-4.6 The system shall flag devices that contact the same destination at regular inter-
             vals with similar-sized messages (possible C2 beaconing).

4.2.5 NIDPS-5: Classification of Attacks (Layer 3)

FR-5.1  The system shall sort every flow into a class using XGBoost or Random Forest.
        The possible classifications are DoS/DDoS, Botnet/C2, Infiltration, Spoofing,
        Reconnaissance, Benign.

FR-5.2 The system shall evaluate each classification and give it a confidence score.

FR-5.3 The system shall label a flow as "unknown" when the model’s confidence is too
             low, instead of forcing it into a class.

4.2.6 NIDPS-6: Decision Fusion

FR-6.1 The system shall combine the results of all three layers to decide whether each
             flow should be passed (PASS), flagged (ALERT) or blocked (BLOCK).

FR-6.2 The system shall record which layers led to each decision and the evidence used.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                       33

FR-6.3  The system shall keep a risk score for each device that rises with suspicious
FR-6.4  activity and drops over time when the device behaves normally.

        The system shall block a device when its risk score reaches the block threshold.

4.2.7 NIDPS-7: Dynamic Blocking

FR-7.1 The system shall block a device that it has deemed risky by placing its IP ad-
             dress/MAC address into a block/blacklist.

FR-7.2 The system shall remove members from the block list once the set cooldown
             timer has passed.

FR-7.3  The system shall have an allow/whitelist, and it shall never block any devices
        on it. It must necessarily include the NIDPS itself, any routers, DNS and admin
        devices as well.

FR-7.4 The system shall double the block time each time the same device is blocked
             again within 24 hours, up to a set maximum.

FR-7.5 The system shall stop blocking and only raise alerts if too many blocks happen
             within one minute, and warn the administrator.

FR-7.6 The system shall log every block, skipped block and release.

4.2.8 NIDPS-8: Recovery Mechanism

FR-8.1 The system shall contain a watchdog per component which it must feed every
             few seconds.

FR-8.2  The system shall assume that a part of the system has failed if it misses some
        amount of watchdog checks or stops running completely, at which point it will
        try to restart it.

FR-8.3 The system shall, upon the failure of a component, switch to one of three modes:
             let all traffic through unfiltered, block all traffic or run with reduced functionality.

FR-8.4 The system shall switch to a low functionality mode when it is overwhelmed
             and switch back when it can handle the load.

FR-8.5 The system shall, upon failure, create logs that detail when it happened, in what
             mode and the affected packets before recovery.

4.2.9 NIDPS-9: Monitoring

FR-9.1 The system shall require the administrator to log in before the dashboard can be
             viewed.

FR-9.2 The system shall show live traffic statistics, alerts, blocks and types of attacks,
             updated at least every two seconds.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS  34

FR-9.3  The system shall show a list of alerts with time, source, destination, attack type,
        layer and confidence, which can be filtered.
FR-9.4
FR-9.5  The system shall show the evidence behind each alert.

FR-9.6  The system shall list blocked devices with the reason and time left, and let the
FR-9.7  administrator unblock them.

        The system shall show traffic and risk scores for each connected device.

        The system shall show the health of each component, along with CPU, memory,
        temperature and uptime.

4.2.10 NIDPS-10: Configurations

FR-10.1 The system shall let the administrator turn each detection layer on or off while
             running.

FR-10.2 The system shall let the administrator choose the failure mode and watchdog
             settings.

FR-10.3 The system shall let the administrator change detection thresholds, block times
             and flow timeouts within safe limits.

FR-10.4 The system shall let the administrator add and remove devices from the allowlist.

FR-10.5 The system shall let the administrator reload the rules and recalibrate the
             anomaly threshold on demand.

FR-10.6 The system shall apply configuration changes without restarting, and log who
             made each change and what was changed.

4.2.11 NIDPS-11: Logging and Storage

FR-11.1 The system shall store any alert notifications, potential alerts and blocks, calcu-
             lated risk scores, configuration data and health statuses in a log file or database.

FR-11.2 The system shall make sure not to store actual packet data during operation.

FR-11.3 The system shall let the admin select a block of time and export alerts, blacklists
             or other operational data in a preferable format such as CSV.

4.2.12 NIDPS-12: Training and Versioning

FR-12.1 The system shall build training data using the same flow extractor that runs on
             the device.

FR-12.2 The system shall train and test models offline.

FR-12.3 The system shall be able to save each model in a package, which will include
             what model it is, the extractor version, classes, feature list and training date.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS             35

FR-12.4 The system shall not be able to load a model that does not follow the flow ex-
             tractor rules and architecture.

FR-12.5 The system shall keep the previous model so the administrator can return to it in
             one step.

4.2.13 NIDPS-13: Auto Maintenance

FR-13.1 The system shall download and test new rules every day, and keep using the old
             rules if the test fails.

FR-13.2 The system shall recalculate and adjust the anomaly threshold by looking at
             traffic at the end of a set time such as after a week.

FR-13.3 The system shall delete records about flows after a set time frame has passed,
             which will be a week by default.

