4.4 Non-Functional Requirements

The NFRs will measure the statistics of how the system will perform. The goal is to allow
the system to run on a Raspberry Pi with around 4 GB RAM or a mid-tier laptop to serve
a home/small office network of 30 or more devices. These are design targets that will be
tested, and adjusted if needed, during the evaluation in Section 5.10.

4.4.1 Performance

    • NFR-1 The system shall only add an upper limit of 200 ms of delay onto devices
       under normal loads.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS  37

    • NFR-2 The system shall be able to support 50 Mbps of traffic with all layers active.
    • NFR-3 The system shall analyze each flow and then assign a risk score to its device

       in no more than 15 ms.
    • NFR-4 The system shall, when it has recognized an anomalous device, block it within

       1 second.
    • NFR-5 The system shall come to a decision regarding long-running connections in

       about a second after it has timed out.

4.4.2 Capacity

    • NFR-6 The system shall support up to 30 devices connected at the same time along
       with 5 people using the dashboard.

4.4.3 Resource Usage

    • NFR-7 The system shall use around a maximum of 70% of a lower-end Pi CPU and
       no more than 4 GB of memory at a given time.

4.4.4 Reliability and Availability

    • NFR-8 The system shall be able to detect failure and start resetting within 3 seconds.
       The system should not take more than 30 seconds to restart in normal conditions.

    • NFR-9 The system shall let all packets pass through it undiagnosed in case fail-open
       mode is activated to not interrupt the connection.

4.4.5 Accuracy

    • NFR-10 The system shall not raise false positives on more than 1% of normal traffic
       and not block any devices during a week of testing.

    • NFR-11 The system shall be able to detect more attacks than Suricata alone using its
       three-layered architecture, measured by macro F1-score.

4.4.6 Security

    • NFR-12 The system shall only allow access to the dashboard from the local network;
       it will store passwords in salted hashes and block in case of many incorrect login
       attempts.

    • NFR-13 The system shall be able to run all detection algorithms without admin priv-
       ileges if they do not require network access.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS  38

4.4.7 Privacy

    • NFR-14 The system shall not save any specific data about packets or flows and delete
       them after a set timeframe (defaults to 1 week).

4.4.8 Usability

    • NFR-15 The system shall be simple enough that a non-technical user can understand
       what caused a failure and unblock it within a few minutes.

    • NFR-16 The system shall have a simplified explanation for the type of attack found,
       layer that detected it and other such information in alerts/logs.

4.4.9 Maintainability

    • NFR-17 The system shall allow new models and rules to be installed, or rolled back,
       without interrupting network traffic.

4.4.10 Extensibility and Reusability

    • NFR-18 The system shall allow a new detector to be added without changing any
       other component.

    • NFR-19 The system shall allow the flow extractor and training pipeline to be reused
       separately from the device.

4.4.11 Portability

    • NFR-20 The system shall be installed with a single script on any 64-bit Debian-based
       ARM or x86 device that meets the hardware requirements in Section 4.7.1.

4.5 Assumptions

The specification is based on the following assumptions. If one of them does not hold, the
affected requirements may need to be revisited.

    • The NIDPS is the only wireless network that client devices use. The ISP router’s
       own Wi-Fi is disabled, and devices connected by cable directly to the ISP router are
       outside the protection of the system.

    • The host is a mid-range laptop or a Raspberry Pi with at least 4 GB of RAM, a
       wireless adapter that supports access point mode and an Ethernet port for the uplink.

    • The ISP router works correctly and provides internet access, but it is not relied on for
       any security function.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                   39

• The NIDPS host and the administrator’s devices are trusted and have not been com-
  promised.

• The administrator is not a security expert. Default settings must therefore be safe
  without tuning.

• Periodic internet access is available for ruleset updates.

• Most traffic is encrypted with TLS or QUIC, so payload inspection is not possible for
  the ML layers. Detection of encrypted traffic relies on flow metadata and handshake
  fingerprints only.

• The public datasets (CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018 and CIC-IoT-2023) and
  their raw PCAP files remain available for downloading.

• Attack traffic for the self-collected dataset is generated only on an isolated network
  owned by the project team, against the team’s own devices.

• Traffic observed during the calibration window is mostly benign; flows flagged by
  Suricata during this window are excluded.

• The household traffic volume does not exceed the measured capacity of the device
  for long periods; short peaks are handled by degraded mode.

