4.8 Graphical User Interface

The NIDPS has one graphical user interface: the web dashboard used by the Network
Administrator. It is served by the NIDPS host over HTTPS and is reachable only from the
protected local management network. Network clients have no interface because detection
and mitigation operate transparently inline. The interface provides a unified, responsive
dashboard layout with a persistent left-hand navigation sidebar, top status header (including
system theme toggle and live connectivity indicators), and dedicated functional panels.
Figure 4.2 shows the proposed navigation flow between the pages.

                        Figure 4.2: Navigation flow of the Network Administrator.

4.8.1 Login Screen

                                            Figure 4.3: Login screen.

Access to the dashboard is strictly gated by administrator authentication (UC-01). The login
interface displays username and password input fields and notifies the operator that access
attempts are audited. Generic error messages are returned upon unsuccessful attempts to
prevent username enumeration, and administrative accounts are temporarily locked after
multiple consecutive failures (NFR-12). Successful authentication establishes a secure ses-
sion cookie, directing the administrator to the Overview page.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS  54

4.8.2 Overview Screen

                                          Figure 4.4: Overview screen.

The Overview screen serves as the primary operational dashboard for the network admin-
istrator (Figure 4.4). The System Summary panel aggregates high-level telemetry, dis-
playing total packets/flows analyzed, total intrusions detected, currently active host blocks,
and overall protection state (OPERATIONAL), alongside runtime details such as the active
ML-IDS engine version, last health check timestamp, and kernel failure mode (fail-open).
The Recent Alerts panel displays a real-time table of recent detection events indicating
timestamp, originating source IP, attack classification, and automated enforcement action
(alert or block), with a direct shortcut to view all alerts. (UC-02)

4.8.3 Network Traffic Screen

The Network Traffic screen provides in-depth visibility into network bandwidth and active
conversations (Figure 4.5). Live counters display current observed throughput (in KB/s),
aggregate bidirectional flows analyzed over the past 24 hours, and the count of connected
devices alongside active concurrent flows. An interactive Traffic History timeline charts
network volume over rolling time windows. The Connected Devices and Flows table fea-
tures real-time search filtering by host or IP, detailing source and destination addresses,
transport protocols, cumulative transferred volume, assessed threat risk level, and last ac-
tivity timestamps. (UC-02)
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                             55

                                      Figure 4.5: Network Traffic screen.

4.8.4 Intrusion Alerts Screen

                    Figure 4.6: Intrusion Alerts screen with the Alert Evidence panel.

The Intrusion Alerts screen functions as the central alert triage console (Figure 4.6). The
Detection Results table lists identified events with filter controls for IP address, attack
category, and verdict. Each entry indicates the timestamp, source IP, destination IP, classifi-
cation (such as port scan, brute force, DoS, SSH probe, or anomalous flow), detecting layer,
and model confidence score. Selecting an alert opens the Alert Evidence drawer, which
displays the calculated host risk score, final verdict, and an explainable breakdown across
all three layers: Layer 1 Suricata signature rule SID, Layer 2 Isolation Forest anomaly
score, and Layer 3 Random Forest/XGBoost classification confidence. (UC-03, UC-04)
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS  56

4.8.5 Blocked Hosts and Allowlist Screen

                              Figure 4.7: Blocked Hosts and Allowlist screen.

The Blocked Hosts and Allowlist screen provides complete administrative control over
prevention and exemption policies (Figure 4.7). The Active Blocks table lists currently
blocked IP addresses, identified host names, detection reason and originating layer, block
initiation timestamp, remaining lockout duration, and a manual [unblock] action button to
release hosts blocked by mistake. The Allowlist section manages entities exempt from au-
tomated blocking, including permanent infrastructure (ISP gateway, NIDPS access point)
and administrator devices, with an input form to add new IP entries. The Prevention Sta-
tus panel reports active blocking engine health, quota consumption against the configured
block rate limit, and default quarantine duration. (UC-05, UC-06)

4.8.6 Detection and Prevention Configuration Screen

The Configuration screen allows the administrator to inspect and update all detection,
mitigation, and model settings at runtime without requiring a service restart. Changes
are validated immediately before persistence, and default values can be restored on de-
mand. The configuration interface is divided into four dedicated tabs shown in Figures 4.8
through 4.11:

• Detection Layers Tab (Figure 4.8): Allows the administrator to independently enable
  or disable individual detection layers—Layer 1 (Suricata signature matching), Layer 2
  (Isolation Forest flow deviation scoring), and Layer 3 (Random Forest / XGBoost attack
  classification). Administrative state is decoupled from runtime daemon health; disabled
  layers are excluded from decision fusion calculations. (UC-08)

• Thresholds Tab (Figure 4.9): Provides controls to tune detection sensitivity, including
  the Isolation Forest anomaly score cutoff (default 0.75), multi-class classifier confidence
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS            57

  threshold (default 0.80), host risk blocking threshold (default 70), and flow inactivity
  timeout (default 60 s). (UC-09)

• Prevention and Failure Tab (Figure 4.10): Configures active mitigation parameters, in-
  cluding initial block duration (30 min), repeat offender penalty escalation multiplier (2×),
  and maximum block rate cap (60/min). It also controls the kernel failure policy among
  Fail-Open (passing traffic if inspection daemons fail), Fail-Closed (blocking traffic upon
  component failure, with explicit confirmation prompt), and Degraded (operating with a
  reduced inspection profile), and displays watchdog operational status. (UC-07, UC-10)

• Rules, Calibration and Models Tab (Figure 4.11): Displays active Suricata ruleset
  metadata (Emerging Threats Open) with an on-demand ruleset reload trigger; Isolation
  Forest baseline calibration date and status with an instant recalibration button; and active
  machine learning model package versions and training provenance. (UC-12)

Figure 4.8: Configuration screen – Detection Layers tab.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                   58

Figure 4.9: Configuration screen – Detection Thresholds tab.

Figure 4.10: Configuration screen – Prevention and Failure tab.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                                          59

                Figure 4.11: Configuration screen – Rules, Calibration and Models tab.

4.8.7 System Health Screen

                                      Figure 4.12: System Health screen.

The System Health screen displays real-time diagnostics and supervisory logs across all
NIDPS subsystems (Figure 4.12). The Component Status panel tracks every background
service (Suricata, flow extractor, Isolation Forest, RF/XGBoost classifier, decision fusion,
prevention engine, and watchdog), confirming operational status (running), last successful
heartbeat check timestamp, and failure counters. The Resource Monitoring panel displays
live gauges for host CPU usage, memory utilization, device operating temperature, and
system uptime. The Failure and Recovery History panel maintains an auditable chrono-
logical record of watchdog validations, automated process restarts, and recovery events.
(UC-11)
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                   60

4.9 Database Design

The NIDPS does not store any direct data such as packets or extra information. It will only
store data points such as hosts, flow records, black/white lists, model packages, configura-
tion state, verdicts and logs for a period. An RDBMS is used as the records are linked to
each other, e.g. blocks happen in response to a verdict on a flow, and so on. The dashboard
view may also need filtered entries. SQLite is chosen because it runs in-process with no
server, stores everything in one file and is sufficient for the write rate of a single home de-
vice. Raw packets and payloads are never stored. Live counters shown on the dashboard
are kept in memory and on the event bus rather than in the database.

4.9.1 ER Diagram

Figure 4.13 shows the entity-relationship diagram. HOST is the central entity: a host has
many flow records, evidence items, verdicts and blocks. Each flow record can have several
evidence items (one per layer) and at most one final verdict, and a BLOCK verdict creates
at most one block entry. Evidence produced by an ML layer refers to the model package
that produced it, and calibration states belong to the anomaly model. Administrative actions
are linked to the admin user who performed them.

Figure 4.13: Entity-relationship diagram of the NIDPS database.
CHAPTER 4. SOFTWARE REQUIREMENT SPECIFICATIONS                                    61

