The primary risks to be aware of in this project are R2 and R3, as they will decide if the
system will be usable and worthwhile in a real network environment. These are countered
by design decisions such as local calibration and cautious decision fusion. They are also
measured directly while evaluating the system so they can be addressed properly.

4.11 Conclusion

This chapter set out what the NIDPS has to do and how well. The 13 features break down
into 63 functional requirements, and 20 non-functional requirements put numbers on quali-
ties such as performance, reliability, accuracy, security and privacy. Twelve use cases show
how the administrator, the scheduler and an attacker interact with the system. The hardware
and software lists, the seven dashboard screens and the twelve-table database give enough
detail to start building. Two risks matter most: R2, models trained on public datasets per-
forming badly on household traffic, and R3, false positives blocking legitimate devices.
Every requirement has an identifier, and Table 6.4 in Chapter 6 links each one to the part of
the design that meets it.
Chapter 5

Proposed Approach and Methodology

This chapter presents the methodology through which the NIDPS detects and blocks mali-
cious traffic on a network. The proposed approach is a hybrid detection framework which
combines three detection layers, whose output goes through a decision fusion algorithm,
that provides a verdict and then an enforcement and self recovery mechanism that acts on
that decision. This chapter covers the datasets and how they are fused, automated preven-
tion and failure handling and the evaluation protocol.

5.1 Overview of the Proposed Framework

The NIDPS runs on a single Linux host that acts as a routers wireless access point. There-
fore, all packets between the client devices and the ISP gateway pass through it. Each
packet travels two independent paths, the inline path in which Suricata inspects packets as
they are being forwarded and can drop them immediately, and an analysis path, in which
packets are grouped into bidirectional flows, identified by 78 distinct features and their TLS
fingerprints. These flow records are scored by an unsupervised Isolation Forest (Layer 2)
and a supervised Random Forest/XGBoost classifier (Layer 3).
The three layers cover different threat classes:

    • Layer 1 – Signature detection (Suricata): known attacks with stable signatures,
       such as SSH brute force, port scanning and DNS spoofing.

    • Layer 2 – Anomaly detection (Isolation Forest): traffic that deviates from the net-
       work’s normal profile, including zero-day behaviour, C2 beaconing and data exfiltra-
       tion.

    • Layer 3 – Supervised classification (Random Forest/XGBoost): assignment of
       suspicious flows to known attack families such as DDoS and lateral movement.

A decision-fusion engine combines the three outputs into one verdict per flow (pass, alert or
block). Block verdicts are enforced through iptables and ipset. A watchdog supervises ev-
ery component and applies the configured failure mode when one of them stops. Figure 5.1
summarises this pipeline.
The design separates what must be fast from what can be thorough. Suricata decides per
packet and adds only microseconds of forwarding delay. The machine learning layers de-
cide per flow, so their verdicts are applied to the remainder of a long flow and to all later
traffic from the offending host.

                                                     69
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                  70

Figure 5.1: Proposed three-layer NIDPS pipeline. The inline packet path is represented by the solid
line, whereas the dotted line represents the analysis and the supervision path.

5.2 Datasets

The machine learning layers are trained on three public intrusion detection benchmarks and
evaluated additionally on traffic captured from our own deployment. The goal is not the
highest score on one benchmark, but a model that still works when the training traffic and
the deployment traffic differ.

5.2.1 Public Benchmark Datasets

Table 5.1 lists the public datasets used for training and cross-evaluation.

Table 5.1: Public benchmark datasets used for training and cross-evaluation.

Dataset       Source          Traffic profile     Attack families relevant
                                                  to this project
CIC-IDS-2017  Canadian        5 days of
[16]          Institute for   enterprise-style    Brute force (SSH/FTP),
              Cybersecurity   traffic             DoS/DDoS, port scan,
CSE-CIC-      (CIC)                               botnet, infiltration
IDS2018 [17]
              CIC with the    Larger AWS-hosted Brute force, DoS/DDoS,
CIC-IoT-2023  Communica-      network, 10 days botnet, infiltration
[18]          tions Security
              Establishment   105 IoT devices in  DDoS, recon/scan,
                              a smart-home        spoofing, brute force,
              CIC             topology            Mirai-style attacks

CIC-IoT-2023 is closest to the target environment because home networks are increasingly
dominated by IoT devices. CIC-IDS-2017 and CSE-CIC-IDS2018 contribute a wider range
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY  71

of host-based attacks. Published analyses of CIC-IDS-2017 have reported labelling and
flow-construction errors in the original CSV files [19], which is one more reason flows are
regenerated from the raw captures (Section 5.2.3).

5.2.2 Self-Collected Real-World Dataset

A fourth dataset is captured on the deployed access point itself. It has two parts:
   1. Benign capture. Normal household or lab traffic (browsing, streaming, video calls,
       IoT devices, software updates) is recorded over several days. Flows that Suricata
       flags during this period are removed before the capture is labelled benign.

   2. Attack capture. Each in-scope attack is executed from a dedicated attacker machine
       in an isolated test network that we own, using standard penetration-testing tools. The
       start and end time, attacker address and target of each run are logged, so every flow
       in the window can be labelled automatically from these logs.

This dataset is used only for testing, never for training. It measures how well models trained
on public data transfer to a real home network.

5.2.3 Feature Harmonisation Across Datasets

The public datasets do not share one feature format. The two CIC-IDS sets ship CI-
CFlowMeter CSVs with about 80 columns whose names and rounding differ between re-
leases, while CIC-IoT-2023 ships a different, smaller feature set computed over packet
windows. A model trained on one format cannot be applied to another, and none of them is
guaranteed to match what the live system computes.
To remove this mismatch, the following procedure is applied:

   1. Download the raw PCAP files of every dataset instead of the provided CSVs.

   2. Run the same flow extractor that runs on the device (Section 5.3.2) over every PCAP.
       All datasets then share an identical 78-feature schema, with identical definitions,
       timeouts and units. The extractor runs with the same timeouts as the device (Ta = 10 s,
       Ti = 30 s), so a training record and a live record always cover the same span of traffic.

   3. Re-label the regenerated flows by matching their 5-tuple and timestamps against each
       dataset’s published attack schedule.

   4. Map every dataset-specific label onto one unified attack taxonomy (Table 5.2).
The key advantage is that training-time and serving-time features are produced by the same
code, so there is no train/serve skew.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                              72

Table 5.2: Unified attack taxonomy used for Layer 3.

Unified class           Examples of original labels            Primary
                                                               detecting
Benign                  BENIGN, BenignTraffic                  layer
Reconnaissance          PortScan, Recon-PortScan,              –
                        Recon-OSScan                           L1, L3
Brute force             SSH-Patator, FTP-Patator,
                        DictionaryBruteForce                   L1, L3
DoS / DDoS              DoS Hulk, DDoS LOIC,
                        DDoS-SYN_Flood, DDoS-UDP_Flood         L1, L3
Botnet / C2             Bot, Mirai variants
Spoofing                DNS_Spoofing, MITM-ArpSpoofing         L2, L3
Infiltration / lateral  Infiltration, internal scanning after  L1
movement                compromise                             L3

5.2.4 Pre-processing

The regenerated flow tables are cleaned before training:

    • CSE-CIC-IDS2018 day selection: only the Friday-02-03-2018 capture (Bot: Zeus
       and Ares) is regenerated from this dataset’s ten captured days. The remaining nine
       would add several hundred gigabytes of raw capture for little extra value: two days
       (Thursday-22-02, Friday-23-02) cover Web/SQL-injection attacks that fall outside
       the unified taxonomy of Table 5.2, and the rest (Brute force, DoS/DDoS, Infiltration)
       are already represented in CIC-IDS-2017 and CIC-IoT-2023. The Bot day is kept
       despite this overlap because it is the only capture, across all three public datasets,
       with genuine bot-to-C2 check-in traffic rather than a flood proxy for "botnet":
       CIC-IoT-2023’s Mirai sub-attacks (Mirai-greeth_flood, Mirai-greip_flood,
       Mirai-udpplain) are GRE/UDP floods, not beaconing traffic, so they do not
       exercise the C2-beaconing detector of Section 5.5.4.

    • Invalid values: rows with infinite or undefined values (for example, rates computed
       over a zero-length flow) are dropped, and exact duplicate rows are removed.

    • Identifier removal: source and destination IP addresses, ports, flow IDs and times-
       tamps are removed from the feature vector. They identify the lab, not the attack,
       and keeping them lets a model memorise the attacker’s address instead of learning
       behaviour.

    • Constant features: features with zero variance in the training set are removed.

    • Splitting: within one dataset, the split into training and test sets is done by capture
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY  73

       day rather than at random, because neighbouring flows of the same attack run are
       nearly identical and a random split leaks them into both sets.

    • Class imbalance: class proportions are heavily skewed, in opposite directions: be-
       nign flows dominate CIC-IDS-2017, while attack flows dominate CIC-IoT-2023.
       Layer 3 uses class weights inversely proportional to class frequency; oversampling
       (SMOTE), if used, is applied only to the training fold.

    • Scaling: tree-based models do not need feature scaling, so values are kept in their
       original units. This also keeps the online pipeline simple.

    • Isolation Forest training set: Layer 2 is trained only on benign flows, since it models
       normal behaviour.

5.3 Traffic Acquisition and Flow Construction

All detection depends on how packets reach the detectors and how they are summarised.
This section describes the inline packet path, the flow assembly algorithm and the extraction
of TLS fingerprints from encrypted traffic.

5.3.1 Inline Packet Path

The host runs hostapd to expose a wireless network and dnsmasq to provide DHCP and
DNS to clients. IP forwarding is enabled and the uplink interface performs NAT towards
the ISP gateway. Forwarded traffic is diverted by an iptables rule in the FORWARD chain
to an NFQUEUE, where Suricata runs in IPS mode. For each packet, Suricata returns an
accept or drop verdict to the kernel.
A copy of every forwarded packet is also read by the flow assembler through a packet
capture socket on the wireless interface. The flow assembler never holds packets back, so a
slow ML layer cannot slow down forwarding.
One platform detail matters for lateral movement. By default, the wireless driver and
hostapd relay frames between two wireless clients directly, without passing them up to
the IP layer, so netfilter and Suricata never see them. To inspect client-to-client traffic,
client isolation (ap_isolate=1) is enabled and intra-network traffic is routed through the
host. Without this setting, lateral movement can only be detected when it crosses the AP’s
IP layer.

5.3.2 Flow Assembly Algorithm

A flow is the set of packets sharing the same 5-tuple (source IP, destination IP, source
port, destination port, protocol) in either direction. The direction of the first packet defines
“forward”; replies are “backward”. Algorithm 5.1 shows how flows are built and closed.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                            74

Algorithm 5.1: Bidirectional flow assembly

    Input: packet stream P; active timeout Ta (default 10 s); idle timeout Ti (default
             30 s)

    Output: completed flow records with 78 features

1 FlowTable ← empty hash map keyed by canonical 5-tuple;

2 foreach packet p ∈ P do

3 k ← canonical(p.5tuple) ;                 // same key for both directions

4 if k ∈/ FlowTable then

5   create flow f with f .start ← p.time and f . f wd ← direction of p;

6   FlowTable[k] ← f ;

7 f ← FlowTable[k];

8 update the running statistics of f with p (direction, size, flags, time);

9 if p carries a TLS ClientHello or ServerHello then

10  attach JA3/JA3S to f (Algorithm 5.2);

11 if p has FIN in both directions or RST then

12  emit( f );

13  remove k;

14 else if p.time − f .start ≥ Ta then

15  emit( f ) as a partial record;

16  reset the counters of f but keep k;

    // run periodically
17 foreach flow f with now − f .last_seen ≥ Ti do
 18 emit( f );
 19 remove its key;

The active timeout lets a long-running connection, such as a slow exfiltration channel, be
scored in segments while it is still open, instead of only when it closes. Each segment is
emitted as its own flow record, after which its counters reset. The idle timeout reclaims
memory from flows that stop without a FIN or RST. Table 5.3 gives the default values.

                                     Table 5.3: Flow timeout parameters.

    Parameter          Default              Effect
    Active timeout Ta
                       10 s (configurable,  An open flow is re-scored every Ta,
                       e.g. 5–30 s)         which caps how long one connection
                                            can run unscored
    Idle timeout Ti    30 s
                                            A flow with no packets for Ti is
                                            closed and scored

Most flows, such as web requests, DNS lookups and scan probes, end in well under 5
seconds and are scored once, when they close. Only long-lived connections are segmented.
Ta trades CPU for reaction time: halving it doubles the scoring work for long flows. The
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                        75

default of 10 s is a conservative starting point for low-cost hardware. Ta is a configuration
parameter, so a deployment with spare CPU can lower it, for example to 5 s, to react faster.
The evaluation measures both settings (Section 5.10.3).
The 78 features follow the CICFlowMeter definitions: packet and byte counts per direction,
packet length statistics, inter-arrival time statistics, TCP flag counts, header lengths, bulk
and sub-flow measures, and active/idle periods. To keep memory constant per flow, means
and variances are updated incrementally with Welford’s method (Equation 5.1) rather than
by storing every packet:

                            µn   =  µn−1  +  xn  − µn−1  ,
                                                   n

                            Mn = Mn−1 + (xn − µn−1)(xn − µn),                       (5.1)

                            σn2  =   Mn   .
                                    n−1

Here xn is the n-th observation (for example, a packet length), µn the running mean and Mn
the running sum of squared deviations. Minimum and maximum are tracked directly.

5.3.3 TLS Fingerprint Extraction (JA3/JA3S)

Most traffic is encrypted, so payload cannot be inspected without decryption. Decrypting
traffic on the AP would require installing a certificate on every client, would break cer-
tificate pinning in many apps and would cost far more CPU than the target hardware can
spare. Instead, the system uses fields of the TLS handshake that are sent in clear text [20],

as shown in Algorithm 5.2.

Algorithm 5.2: JA3/JA3S fingerprint
  Input: TLS ClientHello (or ServerHello)
  Output: 32-character MD5 fingerprint

1 read the TLS version, cipher suites, extension types, supported groups (elliptic

   curves) and EC point formats;

2 remove GREASE values (0x0a0a, 0x1a1a, . . . , 0xfafa) from every list;

3 join the decimal values of each list with “-”;

4 sJA3 ← Version,Ciphers,Extensions,Groups,PointFormats;

5 sJA3S ← Version,Cipher,Extensions ;                          // ServerHello
6 return MD5(s);

The fingerprint identifies the client software that opened the connection, not the user. Mal-
ware and C2 frameworks often use TLS libraries with fingerprints that differ from common

browsers and operating systems. The pair (JA3, JA3S) is attached to the flow record and
used by Layer 2 in two ways: as a rarity signal (how many local hosts have ever used this
fingerprint) and as part of the key for beacon detection (Section 5.5.4). Suricata’s TLS

parser can emit the same hashes in its EVE logs, which lets Layer 1 rules match known-bad
fingerprints directly.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                               76

5.4 Layer 1: Signature-Based Detection

Layer 1 catches attacks that have a known, stable pattern. It is the only layer that acts on
individual packets, so it is the first line of prevention.

5.4.1 Suricata Configuration

Suricata [21] runs in IPS mode on the NFQUEUE with the Emerging Threats Open ruleset.
Rule categories that do not apply to a home network (for example, SCADA protocols) are
disabled to save CPU. Each rule carries an action (alert or drop) and a severity from 1
(highest) to 3. Rules with severity 1 that target in-scope attacks are set to drop; the rest only
alert and are passed on to decision fusion. Every alert is written to Suricata’s EVE JSON
output, which the alert ingestor reads over a Unix socket.

5.4.2 Custom Rules for In-Scope Attacks

The ET ruleset matches known payloads well, but several in-scope attacks are defined by
rate rather than content. These are covered by custom threshold rules, which count match-
ing events per source address within a time window (Table 5.4).

Table 5.4: Custom Layer 1 rules for the in-scope attacks.

Attack           Detection logic                       Example threshold
                                                       (tunable)
SSH brute force  New TCP connections (SYN) to port     ≥ 5 in 60 s
                 22 from one source
Port scan        SYN packets from one source to        ≥ 20 ports in 10 s
                 many distinct destination ports
DNS spoofing     DNS responses to clients whose        any occurrence
                 source is not the AP’s own resolver
Known C2 TLS     JA3 hash on a blocklist of published  any occurrence
client           malicious fingerprints
SYN flood (DoS)  SYN packets to one destination        rate above a
                 without completed handshakes          calibrated limit

The thresholds are starting values that are tuned during evaluation. The DNS spoofing rule
relies on the AP design. Clients receive the AP’s address as their only DNS server through
DHCP, so a legitimate DNS answer always comes from the AP. Any DNS response arriving
at a client from another address is forged or comes from a rogue resolver.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                      77

5.4.3 ARP Spoofing Monitor

Suricata inspects IP traffic and does not match rules against ARP frames, which sit below

the IP layer. ARP spoofing is therefore detected by a small companion sensor that belongs

to Layer 1 and reports into the same alert stream. It uses the DHCP lease table of dnsmasq

as the ground truth for which MAC address owns which IP address (Algorithm 5.3).

Algorithm 5.3: ARP consistency check
  Input: ARP frames on the wireless interface; DHCP lease table L (IP → MAC)

   Output: ARP spoofing alerts

1 Bindings ← L ;                                        // trusted IP-to-MAC map

2 foreach ARP reply or gratuitous ARP a do

3 if a.sender_ip ∈ Bindings and Bindings[a.sender_ip] ̸= a.sender_mac then

4  raise alert ARP_SPOOF (attacker a.sender_mac, victim a.sender_ip);

5 if a.sender_ip = gateway_ip and a.sender_mac ≠ AP_mac then

6  raise alert GATEWAY_IMPERSONATION with severity 1;

7 if more than N unsolicited replies from one MAC within window W then

8  raise alert ARP_FLOOD;

 9 on every DHCP lease change, refresh Bindings from L;

Impersonating the gateway is the classic step before a man-in-the-middle attack, so that
check raises the highest severity.

5.5 Layer 2: Unsupervised Anomaly Detection

Layer 2 flags traffic that looks unlike the network’s normal behaviour, without needing a
label for the attack. It covers what signatures miss: new attacks, variants that change their
signature, C2 beaconing and data exfiltration.

5.5.1 Isolation Forest

Isolation Forest [22] is based on a simple observation: anomalies are few and different,
so they are easier to separate from the rest of the data than normal points are. The model
builds an ensemble of random binary trees. Each tree is grown on a random subsample of
ψ flows by repeatedly choosing a random feature and a random split value between that
feature’s minimum and maximum, until every point is isolated or a height limit of ⌈log2 ψ⌉
is reached.
An anomalous flow is isolated after few splits, so its path length h(x) from root to leaf
is short. Averaging over all trees gives E[h(x)], which is normalised by the average path
length of an unsuccessful search in a binary search tree of ψ points:

   c(ψ            )  =  2H  (ψ  −  1)  −  2(ψ −  1)  ,  H(i) ≈ ln(i) + 0.5772     (5.2)
                                              ψ
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                          78

                                    s(x,  ψ  )  =  2−  E [h(x)]         (5.3)
                                                        c(ψ )

The anomaly score s in Equation 5.3 lies between 0 and 1. Scores close to 1 indicate clear
anomalies; scores well below 0.5 indicate normal points. The model uses t = 100 trees and
ψ = 256, the values recommended by the original authors, and is trained only on benign
flows from the harmonised datasets.
Isolation Forest suits the target hardware because training and scoring are cheap. Scoring a
flow costs t tree walks of at most ⌈log2 ψ⌉ = 8 steps each, regardless of how much training
data was used.

5.5.2 Adaptive Threshold Calibration

The model outputs a continuous score, so a threshold τ is needed to turn it into a decision.

A threshold taken from benchmark traffic does not fit every home network: a household

with game consoles and security cameras produces traffic that a benchmark lab never saw,

which shows up as false positives. The calibration scheme in Algorithm 5.4 adjusts only

the threshold to each deployment; it does not retrain the model.

Algorithm 5.4: Per-network threshold calibration
  Input: trained Isolation Forest M; global threshold τg; calibration window W (e.g.
           24–72 h); percentile p (e.g. 99.5); minimum sample count Nmin; maximum
           shift δ

   Output: calibration state {τ, timestamp, n}

1 S ← 0/ ;

2 foreach flow x completed during W do

3 if x matched any Suricata alert or came from a blocked host then

4           skip x ;                                   // keep the baseline clean

5 add M.score(x) to S;

6 if |S| < Nmin then                      // too little local data: fall back
 7 τ ← τg ;
8 else
 9 τl ← p-th percentile of S;
10 τ ← clip(τl, τg − δ , τg + δ );

11 store {τ, now, |S|} as calibration metadata;
12 repeat periodically (e.g. weekly) as the network’s traffic changes;

Two safeguards protect the calibration. The exclusion step leaves out flows that Layer
1 already considers malicious. The clipping step limits how far the local threshold may
move from the global one, so an attacker who is active during calibration cannot raise the
threshold far enough to hide.
The percentile p directly sets the expected false positive rate on the calibration traffic: with
p = 99.5, about 0.5% of benign local flows would exceed τ. This makes the trade-off
between missed attacks and alert volume an explicit, tunable parameter.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                    79

5.5.3 Data Exfiltration Score

Exfiltration is a property of a host over time, not of one flow. For each internal host h, the
system keeps an exponentially weighted moving average and variance of its outbound bytes
per time bucket bt (60 s by default):

            b¯t = α bt + (1 − α) b¯t−1,  σt2 = α (bt − b¯t−1)2 + (1 − α) σt2−1  (5.4)

A bucket is flagged when bt exceeds the host’s baseline by k standard deviations (k = 4 as a
starting value) and the outbound-to-inbound byte ratio of the host’s flows is also unusually
high. The second condition prevents large uploads that are part of normal two-way activity,
such as video calls, from triggering the alert.
Bucket statistics react only once per bucket, so a second, faster check runs on every active-
timeout segment. If one segment carries more than Umax bytes outbound to a destination the
host has never contacted before, a high-weight evidence item is raised immediately. Umax
is set during calibration (Algorithm 5.4) as a high percentile, for example the 99.9th, of
segment upload sizes in local benign traffic. When this evidence leads to a block, leakage
over a single connection is bounded by roughly the host’s upload rate × (Ta + scoring
delay). At 20 Mbps and the default Ta = 10 s, that is about 25 MB.

5.5.4 C2 Beaconing Score

Infected devices typically contact their command and control server at regular intervals.

Flows are grouped by the key (internal host, destination IP or TLS server name, JA3). For

each group with at least m connections in the observation window, the inter-arrival times

∆t1, . . . , ∆tk between consecutive connections are computed, along with their coefficient of

variation:                                      σ∆t
                                                µ∆t
                                         CV  =                                  (5.5)

Human-driven traffic is irregular (high CV ), while beacons are close to periodic (low CV ),

even with added random jitter. The beacon score combines three signals: a low CV , a low

variance in bytes per connection, and a rare JA3 fingerprint on the local network. A group

is reported when all three point the same way, which keeps legitimate periodic traffic, such

as software update checks from common browsers, from being flagged.

5.6 Layer 3: Supervised Attack Classification

Layer 3 assigns each flow to one of the unified classes of Table 5.2. Where Layer 2 answers
“is this unusual?”, Layer 3 answers “which known kind of attack does this look like?”.
The class label decides the response and makes alerts readable on the dashboard. Two
tree ensembles are trained and compared, and the better one under the hardware budget is
deployed.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                              80

5.6.1 Random Forest

Random Forest [23] trains many decision trees, each on a          b√odotsotfrathpesadmfepaletuorefst.heSptrlaitisnianrge
flows, and at each split considers only a random subset of

chosen to minimise Gini impurity:

                                   G(S)  =  1  −  K    p2k                                (5.6)

                                                  ∑
                                                  k=1

where pk is the share of class k among the flows S at a node. The forest predicts by averaging
the class probabilities of all trees. Bootstrapping and feature subsampling make the trees
disagree in useful ways, so the ensemble is less prone to overfitting than a single tree. This
matters because the model is trained on lab traffic and deployed on a different network.

5.6.2 XGBoost

XGBoost [24] builds trees one after another, each new tree correcting the errors of the
current ensemble. It minimises a regularised objective:

L = ∑ l(yi, yˆi) + ∑ Ω( fm),                      Ω(        f  )  =  γ  T  +  1  λ  ∥w∥2  (5.7)
                                                                              2
i                                  m

Here l is the multi-class log loss, T is the number of leaves of tree f and w its leaf weights.
The γ and λ terms penalise large, complex trees. XGBoost is usually more accurate than
Random Forest on tabular data, but it is more sensitive to hyperparameters and can fit
dataset-specific artefacts more closely, which the cross-dataset evaluation (Section 5.10.2)
is designed to expose.

5.6.3 Training and Model Selection

   1. Train on the harmonised flows with identifiers removed (Section 5.2.4) and using
       class weights to counter the imbalance.

   2. Tune hyperparameters (number of trees, maximum depth, learning rate for XGBoost,
       minimum samples per leaf) with stratified 5-fold cross-validation on the training days
       only.

   3. Select by macro-averaged F1-score rather than accuracy.

   4. Apply a hardware budget: among models within one F1 point of the best, choose the
       one with the lowest per-flow inference time and memory on the target device.

   5. Export the chosen model with its feature list and version number, so the runtime
       refuses a model whose features do not match the extractor.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY  81

5.6.4 Confidence Handling

The classifier produces a probability vector p over K classes. The predicted class is yˆ =
arg max p, with confidence c = max p. If the confidence is low then the flow is unlikely
to fit into any of the classes particularly if the flow is an attack that was not seen during
the model training. For flows with c below a confidence threshold θc , instead a laebl
“unknown” is assigned. The Layer 2 score is passed to the decision fusion module. This
allows refinement of any detected anomalies whilst still allowing a high confidence anomaly
to stand untouched.

5.7 Decision Fusion

The layers may differ in their opinions. An action from a single layer would either block
too much (anomalies alone are prone to noise) or too little (signatures alone fail to detect
novel attacks). Evidence from each layer is combined by host and by flow in the decision
fusion module. The underlying motivation is that if one layer provides strong evidence for
a block, then it will be blocked, but if the evidence is weak then either evidence must be
provided by at least a second layer or the evidence must be accumulative.

5.7.1 Verdict Algorithm

Algorithm 5.5 gives the per-flow verdict, and Figure 5.2 shows the same logic as a
flowchart.

  Algorithm 5.5: Per-flow verdict
    Input: flow f ; Suricata alerts A( f ); anomaly score s( f ); threshold τ; class
             probabilities p( f ); thresholds θblock > θalert > θc
    Output: verdict in {PASS, ALERT, BLOCK} and the attributed layer(s)

 1 if f .src ∈ allowlist then return PASS;
 2 if A( f ) contains a drop rule or severity 1 then return BLOCK, layer L1;
 3 y ← arg max p( f ); c ← max p( f );
 4 if y ≠ Benign and c ≥ θblock then return BLOCK, layer L3;
 5 if s( f ) ≥ τ and y ≠ Benign and c ≥ θalert then return BLOCK, layers L2+L3;
 6 if s( f ) ≥ τ or A( f ) ̸= 0/ or (y ≠ Benign and c ≥ θalert) then
  7 add evidence to the host score of f .src (Section 5.7.2);
  8 return ALERT;

 9 return PASS;

A confident known attack is blocked on Layer 3 alone, while a less confident classification
is blocked only when Layer 2 agrees.
