      Figure 5.2: Decision-fusion flow for one flow record.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY  83

5.7.2 Host Evidence Accumulation

Some attacks, such as a slow port scan or low-volume exfiltration, never produce one strong
signal. Their individual flows only trigger alerts. The host score Sh collects this weak
evidence and lets it fade over time, so that a single stray alert does not lead to a block but a
steady stream of alerts does:

Sh(t) = Sh(tprev) e−λ (t−tprev) + wv          (5.8)

Here wv is the weight of the new alert (for example, higher for a Suricata alert than for

an anomaly score just above τ) and λ is a decay rate. With a half-life of 10 minutes,
λ = ln 2/600 s−1. When Sh reaches the block threshold Θ, the host is blocked. The same
score is shown on the dashboard as the host’s current risk level.

5.7.3 Timing of ML Verdicts

Layers 2 and 3 score completed flows or active-timeout segments (Algorithm 5.1), so their
verdict comes after part of the traffic has passed. Their blocks therefore apply to the rest of
a long flow and to all later traffic from that source. This is acceptable for the attacks they
target: beaconing, exfiltration, DDoS and lateral movement all unfold over many flows.
Attacks that must be stopped at the first packet are the responsibility of Layer 1.
With the default Ta = 10 s, the worst-case delay for an ML verdict on a long flow is about
10 seconds plus scoring time, which is milliseconds for tree models. The data that can leave
over one connection before a block is therefore roughly the upload rate × that delay. This
exposure is measured directly in the live exfiltration tests as “bytes leaked before block”
(Table 5.7), rather than estimated.

5.8 Automated Prevention

A BLOCK verdict is turned into a firewall entry by the enforcer. The enforcer must stop at-
tacks quickly without ever locking the owner out of their own network, so blocking follows
a strict procedure.

5.8.1 Blocking Mechanism

Blocked addresses are stored in an ipset with a per-entry timeout, referenced by a single
iptables rule placed before the NFQUEUE rule in the FORWARD chain (and in INPUT, to
protect the AP itself). Adding or removing an address changes only the set, not the rule list,
so a block takes effect immediately and costs the same regardless of how many addresses
are blocked. Entries expire automatically, so a block never outlives its intended duration
even if the enforcer crashes.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                        84

5.8.2 Blocking Procedure

Algorithm 5.6 gives the procedure followed for every BLOCK verdict.

  Algorithm 5.6: Enforce a BLOCK verdict
    Input: offending address a; attack class y; attributed layer(s)
    Output: ipset entry and audit record

 1 if a ∈ allowlist (AP, gateway, DNS resolver, admin devices) then
  2 log “block suppressed”;
  3 return;

4 if blocks issued in the last minute ≥ Rmax then
5 downgrade to ALERT;
6 raise a “block rate limit” warning;
7 return;

8 n ← number of earlier blocks of a within 24 h;

9 duration ← min(D0 · 2n, Dmax) ;                  // e.g. D0 = 5 min, Dmax = 24 h

10 add a to the ipset with timeout duration;

11 write audit record {a, y, layers, duration, evidence, time};

12 push the event to the dashboard;

The allowlist check prevents the system from cutting off its own infrastructure, even when
an attacker forges packets from those addresses. The rate limit stops a flood of false posi-
tives, or an attacker spoofing many sources, from turning the NIDPS into a denial-of-service
tool against its own users. Exponential back-off means a first offence is blocked briefly, in
case it was a false positive, while repeat offenders are blocked for longer. Every block can
be lifted manually from the dashboard.

5.9 Fault Tolerance and Failure Handling

An inline security device is itself a single point of failure: if Suricata crashes while traffic
is queued to it, the whole network can go down. The NIDPS therefore defines exactly what
happens when each component fails, and lets the owner choose between availability and
security.

5.9.1 Health Monitoring

Every component (Suricata, flow assembler, ML scorer, decision engine, enforcer) runs as
a separate systemd service. Each one sends a heartbeat to its own watchdog every second,
along with load indicators such as queue depth and processing lag. A component is declared
failed after k = 3 missed heartbeats or if its process exits. systemd restarts failed services
automatically, and its own watchdog timer restarts services that are running but frozen.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                                  85

5.9.2 Failure Modes

Table 5.5 lists the configurable failure modes.

             Table 5.5: Configurable failure modes.

Mode         When Layer 1 fails    When an ML layer fails    Suited to
Fail-open
             Packets bypass the    Remaining layers          Homes where
Fail-closed  queue and are         continue; affected flows  internet access
             forwarded             are logged as unscored    matters most
Degraded     uninspected
(load-                             Flows from unknown        Small offices
shedding)    Forwarded traffic is  hosts are held at ALERT   handling
             dropped until         and new block decisions   sensitive data
             recovery; admin       rely on Layer 1 only
             access to the AP
             stays open            Only a sample of flows    Temporary
                                   (1 in k) is scored,       overload rather
             Non-essential rule    prioritising new and      than a crash
             groups are disabled   high-risk hosts
             to reduce load

Fail-open is implemented in the kernel rather than in software: the NFQUEUE rule is
created with the queue-bypass option, so when no process is attached to the queue, the
kernel accepts packets instead of dropping them. Fail-closed omits this option. Because the
kernel enforces the mode, it holds even when the watchdog itself has crashed.

5.9.3 State Machine

Figure 5.3 shows the states each supervised component moves through.
Two thresholds (high and low watermarks) are used for entering and leaving the degraded
state. Without this gap, a load hovering around one value would cause the system to switch
modes back and forth many times per second. During every failure, the watchdog records
when it was detected, which mode was applied, when service resumed and how many
packets were dropped or bypassed. These records produce the recovery measurements
reported in the evaluation.

5.10 Evaluation Methodology

The system is evaluated on two questions: how well it detects attacks, and whether it can
do so inline on constrained hardware without harming the network.
CHAPTER 5. PROPOSED APPROACH AND METHODOLOGY                       86

Figure 5.3: Watchdog state machine for each supervised component.

5.10.1 Detection Metrics

For every layer and for the fused system, precision, recall, F1-score and false positive rate
(FPR) are reported per class and macro-averaged:

Precision =  TP               Recall = T P ,
                    ,
      TP+FP                            TP+FN
      2 · Precision · Recall  FPR = FP                             (5.9)
F1 = Precision + Recall ,
                                       FP+TN

False positive rate receives particular attention. On a home network producing thousands
of flows per hour, even a 1% false positive rate means dozens of wrong alerts or blocks
every hour. For Layer 2, the area under the precision-recall curve is also reported, since
it summarises performance across all thresholds and is more informative than ROC-AUC
when attacks are rare.

5.10.2 Cross-Dataset Generalisation

Each model is trained on one dataset and tested on every other dataset, giving the matrix
in Table 5.6. The diagonal (train and test on the same dataset, split by day) shows in-
distribution performance. The off-diagonal cells show how much performance drops when
the network changes, which is the realistic situation for a deployed device. Finally, models
trained on all three public datasets combined are tested on the self-collected dataset.
