Every attack in the scope is replayed live against test devices on the isolated lab network
while background benign traffic runs. This end-to-end test checks the complete chain, from
detection through fusion to blocking, rather than each model in isolation.

5.11 Conclusion

The proposed Hybrid framework and the algorithms of the backend were introduced in this
chapter. Known attacks are detected at the packet level, and at the fastest rate, by Suricata
and an ARP consistency check. Isolation Forest with per network and with Host level scores
for exfiltration and C2 beaconing detects unusual behaviour. A Random Forest or XGBoost
classifier is used to classify the flows into known attack families. The analysis of the en-
crypted traffic is done using JA3/JA3S fingerprints and flow stats, without decrypting it, and
all datasets are reconstructed with the device’s flow extractor, so that features are identical
during training and runtime. These signals are converted to verdicts by a decision-fusion
algorithm, which are enforced by a host evidence accumulation, using ipsets with timing
constraints and a mechanism to prevent self-inflicted outages, and applied by a watchdog,
with a configurable failure mode backed by the kernel. The evaluation protocol not only
tests the quality of the detection per dataset, but also the latency, throughput and recovery
of the system on real hardware.
Chapter 6

High-Level and Low-Level Design

This chapter describes how the methodology of Chapter 5 is realised as software. It moves
from the high level to the low level: first an overview of the system and the considerations
that shaped its design, then the architecture of the whole system and of each subsystem, the
strategies behind the main architectural decisions, the class diagram, and finally the policies
and tactics that govern implementation, testing and maintenance.

6.1 System Overview

The NIDPS is a self-contained security appliance built from software on a single Linux
host. The host replaces the role of the home router’s wireless network: clients connect to
the NIDPS access point, and the NIDPS routes their traffic, through its own NAT, to the
existing ISP router, which keeps handling the internet connection. No change is needed on
client devices or on the ISP router.
Functionally, the system does five things:

   1. Provides network access: A wireless access point with DHCP, DNS and routing.
   2. Inspects traffic: Inline signature inspection of every packet, plus flow-level analysis

       of all traffic, including encrypted traffic through metadata.
   3. Decides: Fuses signature, anomaly and classification results into a per-flow verdict

       and a per-host risk score.
   4. Responds: Blocks offending addresses automatically for a limited, escalating time.
   5. Reports and stays available: Shows live statistics and alerts on a web dashboard,

       and keeps the network in a defined state when a component fails.
The system is made up of independent services connected by a local event bus, rather than
as one large program. The packet-forwarding path is kept as short as possible (kernel plus
Suricata), and everything slower like machine learning, the database and the dashboard, sits
beside that path instead of in it. Sections 6.3 and 6.4 explain this choice in detail.

6.2 Design Considerations

This section records the assumptions, constraints, goals and development methods that
shaped the design.

                                                     89
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  90

6.2.1 Assumptions and Dependencies

    • Hardware: The host has at least a quad-core 64-bit ARM or x86 CPU, 4 GB of
       RAM, a wireless adapter that supports AP mode and an Ethernet port for the uplink.

    • Operating system: Debian based Linux distribution with systemd, iptables (use of
       nftables is accepted, but not required), ipset (use of NFQUEUE is accepted, but not
       required) and support in the kernel.

    • Network position: NIDPS is the only wireless network clients access. Devices
       which are connected directly to the ISP router via cable or directly by the ISP router’s
       Wi-Fi are not protected. Turning off the ISP router’s WiFi is a part of the set-up pro-
       cess.

    • Third-party software: Suricata 7 or later with Emerging Threats Open ruleset,
       hostapd, dnsmasq, Python 3.11+ with scikit-learn and XGBoost. Periodic Internet
       access is required for ruleset updates.

    • End users: Owner is NOT a security expert. Defaults should be safe and not require
       tuning, and dashboard should provide information about alerts in easy-to-understand
       terms.

    • Trust: The NIDPS host and the admin’s devices are trusted. The ISP router is as-
       sumed to be working, but is not trusted to provide any security.

    • Likely changes: Changes are likely to include new attack types and new rules, mod-
       els may be retrained and the TLS fingerprinting method could change from JA3 to its
       successor JA4, as TLS client libraries start to shuffle the order of extensions. Each of
       these is isolated by the design behind its component.

6.2.2 General Constraints

Table 6.1 lists the global constraints and their impact on the design.
