6.2.3 Goals and Guidelines

   1. Never make the network worse than a plain router: Every design choice is
       checked against this. It is why fail-open is the default, why blocks expire and why
       the number of blocks per minute is capped.

   2. Keep the fast path short: Only the kernel and Suricata sit in the forwarding path.
       Anything that can be done after the fact is moved off it.

   3. Defence in depth: Each layer covers a weakness of another layer as no single detec-
       tor is trusted to catch everything (Section 5.1).

   4. Speed over memory where the two conflict, within a fixed budget: In order to fit
       the hardware, the model sizes are capped and loaded fully in memory to avoid disk
       access during anlysis.
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  92

   5. Explainable decisions: Every block records which layer decided, why, and with
       what evidence, so the owner can trust or override it.

   6. Same code for training and serving: Features are computed by one implementation
       to rule out train/serve skew.

   7. Configurable, safe defaults: Thresholds, failure mode and block durations are con-
       figurable, but the defaults work by default.

6.2.4 Development Methods

The project follows an iterative, incremental approach in short sprints of two weeks, aligned
with the FYP milestones. Each increment delivers a working vertical slice: first the access
point with Suricata (Layer 1, completed), then flow extraction and Layer 2, then Layer 3,
then fusion and enforcement, and finally the watchdog and dashboard. This order means
the system protects the network from the first increment onwards and every later layer can
be measured against a working baseline.
The machine learning work follows the CRISP-DM cycle (data understanding, preparation,
modelling, evaluation, deployment), because model quality depends on several rounds of
data cleaning and re-evaluation. A plain waterfall model was considered and rejected:
detection performance on real traffic cannot be known in advance, so requirements such as
thresholds and feature sets must be allowed to change as results come in.

6.3 System Architecture

The system is decomposed into five runtime subsystems on the NIDPS host and one offline
subsystem used for training. Figure 6.1 shows the external architecture (how the NIDPS sits
between clients, the ISP router and the administrator) together with the internal subsystems
and their main data flows.
The responsibilities are partitioned as shown in Table 6.2.
There are two ways in which the subsystems work together. The forwarding path is en-
tirely in kernel and Suricata and there is only one feedback loop from the enforcer to ipset.
Everything else is communicated via the local event bus: detectors publish evidence, de-
cision engine consumes it and publishes verdicts, and the rest (dashboard and event store)
subscribe to it.

