The rule order matters: the ipset check comes before the queue, so packets from blocked
hosts are discarded in the kernel and never cost Suricata any CPU. During a DDoS from a
blocked source, this keeps the inspection engine free for other traffic.

6.3.1.2 Detection Subsystem

There are four producers in the detection subsystem. Suricata and the ARP monitor issue
alerts directly. The flow extractor will publish flow records, and the ML scorer will en-
hance the flow records and publish them. The ML scorer consists of three sub-components
internally:

    • Feature validator: Validates each flow record against the feature schema contained
       in the model and rejects the flow record if it does not match or is malformed.

    • Flow models: Once loaded at start-up, they are applied to each flow record, accord-
       ing to the Isolation Forest and the RF/XGBoost classifier.

    • Host behaviour tracker: Maintains per-host state (baseline of exfiltrated bytes,
       timestamps of connections, local JA3 counts) and calculates the exfiltration and bea-
       coning scores of Sections 5.5.3 and 5.5.4 respectively. Its state is bounded: When
       inactive host/keys are entered, they expire.
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  95

6.3.1.3 Decision and Response Subsystem

The decision engine consumes all evidence from the bus, joins Suricata alerts with flow
records by flow identifier and time, runs Algorithm 5.5 and maintains the host scores of
Section 5.7.2. The enforcer receives BLOCK verdicts and runs Algorithm 5.6. The two are
kept separate so that the component allowed to change firewall state is small, simple and
easy to audit.

6.3.1.4 Reliability Subsystem

While the watchdog has application-level health (queue depth, processing lag) information
and determines when to switch to degraded mode, systemd has process level supervision
(automatic restart, start order, resource limits). The Failure mode at the kernel level (queue
bypass on or off) is set during start up of the data plane and is not dependent on the presence
of the watchdog.

6.3.1.5 Management and Reporting Subsystem

The Dashboard API is only available on the LAN side. It counts live flows per second,
alerts and blocks per layer, shows an alert list with the evidence used for the verdict, has a
list of currently blocked hosts with an unblock action, presents the host risk scores, displays
the health status of the components and allows you to set thresholds, failure mode and an
allowlist. Configurations are checked, saved and posted on the bus for running services to
get.

6.3.1.6 Offline Training Pipeline

Figure 6.3 shows the offline pipeline and the hand-over of models to the device.

                Figure 6.3: Offline training pipeline and model hand-over to the device.

Training runs on a workstation, not on the NIDPS host, because it needs far more memory
than inference. The only hand-over to the device is a model package containing the model
file, its feature schema and a version manifest.
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  96

6.4 Architectural Strategies

Each strategy below notes the decision made, the rationale for the decision and what was
rejected.

6.4.1 Layered Defence with a Short Fast Path

Decision: Detection is divided into a packet-level layer on the forwarding path (Suricata)
and flow-level layers off the forwarding path (ML), with a fusion layer.
Reasoning: ML inference on each of the forwarded packets would make it impossible to
stay within the CPU budget, and would introduce additional latency to each connection,
contradicting the first objective of Section 6.2.3. The only forwarding latency cost for flow-
level ML outside the path will be the cost of the Suricata rule that must be applied to the
first packet.
Alternatives rejected: ML-only inline detection (too slow, and no immediate protection
against known attacks); Suricata-only detection (misses zero-day and behavioural attacks).

6.4.2 Metadata-Only Analysis of Encrypted Traffic

Decision: Encrypted traffic analysed only using JA3/JA3S fingerprints and flow statistics.
Reasoning: TLS interception would involve trusted root certificate on each client, would
not be compatible with certificate pinning, would break some IoT devices and would in-
crease CPU load. It would also allow for all the traffic to be displayed in plain text from all
devices in the household, which would be a huge privacy hazard.
Alternatives rejected: Full TLS interception (above); ignoring encrypted traffic (most C2
now uses TLS). Known limitation: browsers now randomise TLS extension order making
JA3 less stable for browser traffic. Since the fingerprinting is separated from the rest of
the flow extractor, other components can remain the same and JA4 can be changed to sort
extensions before hashing.

6.4.3 Offline Training, Online Inference

Decision: Models to be trained on a workstation and deployed as read-only versioned
packages. The device can only do inference and threshold calibration.
Reasoning: A training process of hundreds of millions of flows requires much more mem-
ory than the device available. Online retraining on a device will also enable an adversary to
slowly condition the model to think that malicious traffic is acceptable (model poisoning).
Calibrating just the threshold (Section 5.5.2) adapts to the local network with limited risk.
Alternatives rejected: Continuous online learning (poisoning risk, cost of hardware); a
fixed global threshold (false positives with real home traffic).
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  97

6.4.4 Local Services Connected by an Event Bus

Decision: Each component is its own systemd service and message exchange is handled
via a local publish/subscribe bus. Redis Streams is the proposed implementation.
Reasoning: The failure modes require service isolation, which is where one service can
crash without impacting the others, and where systemd can restart each service indepen-
dently. The bus separates the producers from the consumers. Redis Streams is lightweight,
has a bounded backlog which a restarted consumer can resume from and allows for con-
sumer groups that run the dashboard and the decision engine independently, reading the
same events.
Alternatives rejected: Direct function calls in one process (no fault isolation); Apache
Kafka (too heavy for the hardware); plain unix sockets between each pair of components
(simple, but every new consumer requires new wiring, and while a consumer restarts, mes-
sages are lost).

6.4.5 Kernel-Enforced Blocking and Failure Semantics

Decision: Blocking and failure (ipset with timeouts, and NFQUEUE bypass or not) are
both handled by the kernel and not user space software.
Reasoning: User space programs can crash but the kernel continues to run. While the
queue-bypass flag applies the selected mode regardless of whether the watchdog is down
or not, timed ipsets entries expire even if the enforcer fails.
Alternatives rejected: Add iptables rules one per blocked host (slow when many hosts are
blocked and iptables rules are still there after a crash); A user-space proxy that drops traffic
(adds latency and is itself a crash risk).

6.4.6 Choice of Languages and Products

