6.4.7 Error Detection and Recovery

There are three levels at which errors are dealt with.
   1. Record level: Malformed flow records or alerts are rejected by the feature validator
       and counted, never passed to models.

   2. Component level: Heartbeats and systemd supervision detect crashes and hangs and
       restart the component (Section 5.9).

   3. System level: The system level (kernel-enforced failure mode) determines the fate
       of traffic when a component is down. All three levels will log to the event store and
       hence the dashboard will display when the system was not completely protected.
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  99

6.4.8 Data Storage, Persistence and Retention

We only store derived data: block information, risk history per host, calibration state and
configuration, decisions, alarms and verdicts. No packet data or payload is stored on normal
operation. Flow data is stored in a relatively short rolling window (e.g. 7 days) for analysis
and calibration purposes before being purged. This provides an effective level of privacy
for domestic users whilst placing a reasonable amount of data on a small storage device.

6.4.9 Future Extension

The system is structured to make the most probable extensions accomplished by adding
functionality rather than modifying functionality. A new detector emits to the bus. A new
response (e.g. notification to the owner’s phone) subscribes to decisions. A new extractor
for support of a new JA4 or new model is added.

6.5 Domain Model/Class Diagram

Figure 6.4 shows the main classes of the runtime system. A Flow is the core class that
passes along its properties and TLS fingerprint if present. Each detector creates Evidence
and all implement the same Detector interface. Evidence is converted into a Verdict by
the DecisionEngine and BLOCK verdicts are converted into BlockEntry entries by the En-
forcer.
The Detector interface is the main extension point. A new detection technique is added by
implementing it and publishing its Evidence, without changes to the DecisionEngine. The
Enforcer is the only class that changes firewall state, which keeps that responsibility in one
small, auditable place.

6.6 Policies and Tactics

The following policies only influence aspects of the implementation and do not change the
overall structure of the system.

6.6.1 Rule and Threshold Management

A scheduled job updates the ET Open ruleset every day, it is syntax tested with Suricata’s
test mode and loaded with a live rule reload, so there will never be any inspection that
doesn’t get done. In case of failure, the last ruleset is still in effect. Custom rules are
stored in a separate file in version control, and are never overwritten by changes to ET
rules. All numeric thresholds (rule thresholds, flow timeouts Ta and Ti, τ, θ values, Θ,
block durations) are read from one configuration file with documented defaults.
