6.6.2 Model Packaging and Versioning

Each model package contains the model file, the list of features ordered by version of the
extractor, the class list and the training date and metrics. The ML scorer rejects any pack-
age, whose feature list is incompatible with the running extractor, at load time. XGBoost
models are stored in their native JSON format, not as Python pickles, as loading a pickle
can run arbitrary code. scikit-learn models are stored in joblib files, and only loaded from
the registry directory which is writable only by the deployment user. The last package is
preserved for rollback in case of a bad model.

6.6.3 Engineering Trade-offs

    • Detection speed vs. accuracy: ML verdicts are per flow, not per packet (Sec-
       tion 5.7.3), sacrificing a short delay for ML-detected attacks and eliminating any
       additional forward delay.

    • False positives vs. missed attacks: The calibration percentile p and the block thresh-
       olds are the explicit controls. Defaults prefer fewer false positives as the loss of trust
       on a home network is fast.

    • Availability vs. security: Selected at deployment time (failure mode), default is fail-
       open for homes.

6.6.4 Coding Guidelines and Conventions

Python code adheres to PEP 8, uses type hints and runs through a linter and type-checker as
part of the continuous integration. Each service is a separate package with one exposed en-
try point. All event bus messages are versioned JSON with a schema. This allows producers
and consumers to be upgraded separately. All logging is structured JSON with timestamp,
component name and identifier for either a flow or host. This enables events to be followed
through services.

6.6.5 Requirements Traceability

Every goal from Chapter 2 is mapped to the components that implement it and the tests that
verify it, in a traceability matrix kept in the repository and updated at every sprint review
(Table 6.4).
