6.6.8 Build and Deployment

The system is installed on a clean Debian image with one installation script, which installs
system packages (Suricata, hostapd, dnsmasq, ipset, Redis), creates a Python virtual envi-
ronment with pinned versions of dependencies, copies the configuration and active model
package, and enables the systemd units in dependency order: data plane first, then Suricata,
and then the rest. Only one service is upgraded at a time and that one service is restarted.
Each service is supervised and the failure mode is enforced at kernel level, so that an up-
grade does not leave the network with no behaviour.

6.6.9 Maintenance

Routine maintenance is automated: daily rule updates, weekly threshold recalibration (see
Algorithm 5.4), and purging logs and flow-records outside of the retention window. Models
are retrained offline when new datasets are available, or the dashboard indicates that the
alert rate is increasing that is not explained by calibration, or when new classes of attacks
become available. The health history report on the Dashboard indicates to the owner when
the system was operating in a degraded or failed condition.

6.7 Conclusion

In this chapter we translated the methodology of Chapter 5 into a concrete design. The
NIDPS is a single Linux host, with five runtime subsystems (data plane, detection, decision
and response, management and reporting, and reliability), and an offline training pipeline.
The main architectural decisions are that the forwarding path is short, encrypted traffic is
analysed only via metadata, that training is done offline and thresholds are be calibrated
online, that components are separated with the supervised services communicating with
CHAPTER 6. HIGH-LEVEL AND LOW-LEVEL DESIGN  104

each other via an event bus, and that blocking and failure behaviour is controlled by the
kernel. The class diagram establishes a common Detector interface which allows quick
addition of new detectors, and restricts the number of firewall changes to only one Enforcer.
The policies relating to rule and model management, testing, traceability and deployment
complete the design, and link each policy goal with the component responsible for it and
the test for it.
Chapter 7

Implementation and Test Cases

This chapter shows how the design in Chapter 6 became working software. Most of the
system is finished: the shared foundations used by every service, the flow extractor, both
Layer 1 detectors (the Suricata bridge and the ARP monitor), the Layer 2 anomaly service
with its Isolation Forest model, and the scripts for building the self-collected dataset. The
decision engine and the dashboard are partly built. The classifier, enforcer and watchdog
are not written yet. Everything unfinished is listed in its own section, so no part of this
chapter describes code that does not exist.

7.1 Implementation

Each subsection describes what a component does, which libraries it uses and why we built
it that way. Program code is left out.

7.1.1 Platform and Tools

The services are written in Python 3.11 and run as separate processes. Scapy captures
the packets, Redis Streams carries messages between services, SQLite stores flow records,
settings and calibration state, and scikit-learn provides the Isolation Forest. Suricata is the
only part not written in Python: it runs as its own process and the NIDPS reads its alert log.
Table 7.1 lists the tools. Capturing packets needs raw socket access, so the flow extractor
and the ARP monitor run as root or with the CAP_NET_RAW capability.

