7.1.3 Communication Between Services

The services do not call each other. They talk through Redis Streams. There are five
streams: one for flow-ready notices, one for evidence from the detectors, and one each for
verdicts, configuration changes and heartbeats. Nothing uses the last three yet, because the
decision engine and the watchdog are not built. A small bus class wraps the Redis calls.
A service publishes a message to a stream and reads through a consumer group, and it
acknowledges a message only after handling it. A service that restarts therefore carries on
from where its group stopped instead of losing messages.
Every message is a JSON envelope with a schema version, a unique id, a UTC timestamp,
a type and a payload. A reader rejects a schema version it does not know, so a producer and
a consumer can be upgraded one at a time. A flow-ready message holds only the flow id,
the host address and a partial-flow flag. The 78 features stay in SQLite and are read back
by id, which keeps messages small.
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES  109

7.1.4 Shared Foundations

7.1.4.1 Database

The schema has the twelve tables of the data dictionary (Section 4.9.2), plus indexes on the
columns queried most often: host and start time for flows, host for evidence, and host and
status for blocks. Every service opens the database through the same function. It turns on
foreign keys, creates any missing tables and checks the schema version. Because creating
tables is safe to repeat, whichever service starts first creates the file. Timestamps are stored
as UTC text in ISO 8601 form, so comparing them as strings gives time order, and the
retention purge depends on that.

7.1.4.2 Configuration

The 40 tunable settings are declared in one schema, each with a type, a default and an
allowed range or set of values. Seeding copies the defaults into the database but never
overwrites a value the administrator has changed. A change is checked against its range,
saved with the user and time, and written to the audit log in the same transaction (FR-10.6).
Services cache settings for five seconds, so a new threshold takes effect within seconds and
needs no restart.

7.1.4.3 Detector Interface and Logging

One module defines the value types shared by the services: flow, features, evidence, verdict,
block entry and component health. Evidence has a strength between 0 and 1 and a separate
weight, which the decision engine adds to a host’s risk score. Each service fills in the weight
from the settings before it publishes. Every detector implements the same interface, which
has two methods: one analyses an input and returns zero or more pieces of evidence, the
other reports the detector’s health. The ARP monitor, the Suricata bridge and the anomaly
detector all implement it, so a new detector can be added without touching the decision
engine (Figure 6.4). Each service writes one JSON object per line to standard output, and
the systemd journal collects them in one format.

7.1.5 Flow Extraction Service

7.1.5.1 Packet Capture

The service captures with Scapy on the configured interface and does not keep packets in
memory. Each IPv4 TCP or UDP packet is reduced to a small record holding the timestamp,
addresses, ports, protocol, sizes, TCP flags and window. Scapy is used only in this step, so
the rest of the extractor can be tested with hand-made records. A flow is identified by its
5-tuple, with the two endpoints ordered so that both directions of a conversation share one
key. The first packet seen defines the forward direction.
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES  110

7.1.5.2 Flow Assembly

Active flows sit in a table keyed by the 5-tuple. Each packet updates its flow, and the
assembler then applies the rules of Algorithm 5.1. A flow ends when both sides have sent
FIN or when an RST arrives. If a flow stays open past the active timeout (10 s by default),
it is emitted as a partial flow and a new segment starts under the same key. That way a
long upload is analysed while it happens, not after it ends. About once a second, an idle
check emits every flow that has been silent for the idle timeout (30 s by default). These are
marked partial because they never closed cleanly. If a segment starts after a rollover and
then receives no more packets, it is dropped, so the service never emits an all-zero record.
An error on one packet is logged and the loop carries on.

7.1.5.3 Feature Computation

The 78 features are computed in a single pass as packets arrive, and the packets themselves
are not stored (NFR-14). For packet lengths, inter-arrival times, header lengths and ac-
tive and idle periods, the extractor keeps running statistics updated with Welford’s method
(Equation 5.1), so memory per flow stays constant. It also counts TCP flags and records the
initial window sizes. When a flow is emitted, the values are put in a fixed order. Rates are
divided by the flow duration, with a floor of one microsecond so that single-packet flows
do not divide by zero. A gap of one second or more between packets ends an active burst
and counts as an idle period.
The feature names and groups are modelled on CICFlowMeter so the inputs look familiar.
Our values are single-pass statistics and will not match the published dataset files exactly.
That matters little, because we regenerate the public datasets from their raw captures with
this same extractor (Section 5.2.3).

7.1.5.4 TLS Fingerprints

The extractor reads TLS hellos by hand from the raw bytes. When a TCP payload starts with
a handshake record, it checks for a ClientHello or ServerHello and parses it with Python’s
struct module. This avoids Scapy’s TLS layer and the extra dependencies it needs. A
hello split across several segments is not reassembled. JA3 and JA3S fingerprints follow
Algorithm 5.2: GREASE values are removed, each field list is written as decimal numbers
joined by hyphens, the fields are joined by commas, and the result is hashed with MD5.
The ClientHello parser also reads the server name. A truncated or malformed hello gives
an empty default rather than an exception, so a damaged packet cannot stop capture. All of
this sits behind one function, so moving from JA3 to JA4 means changing one file.

7.1.5.5 Storage and Retention

Each emitted flow is stored with its 5-tuple, UTC start and end times, segment number,
fingerprints, server name, feature list and extractor version. A feature vector that does not
have exactly 78 values is rejected. The 5-tuple is kept for investigation and is not a model
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES  111

storing, the service publishes a flow-ready message. It deletes records older than the reten-
tion window (7 days by default) about once a minute (FR-13.3), and it publishes a heartbeat
at a set interval.
Two limits apply. The idle check, the deletion and the heartbeat are all triggered by arriving
packets, so on a silent network none of them run. And the host record is created from the
source address of the first packet, which for an inbound connection is an outside host.

7.1.6 Layer 1: Signature Detection and ARP Monitoring

7.1.6.1 Suricata Rules

The Suricata setup in the repository is the one we used for the lab captures. It watches the
lab’s host-only network in passive IDS mode, writes alerts and flow records to a JSON log,
and loads only our custom rules. The Emerging Threats ruleset can be added with suricata-
update but is switched off by default. Inline blocking through NFQUEUE belongs to the
data plane on the device and is not part of the repository.
Four custom rules are active, and each counts events per address inside a time window.
SSH brute force is five SYNs to port 22 from one source within 60 s. A port scan is SYNs
to twenty ports within 10 s. A SYN flood is 100 SYNs to one destination within 10 s. DNS
spoofing is any DNS reply from a source other than the access point’s resolver. That rule
contains the lab resolver’s address, so it must be edited for each deployment. A fifth rule,
for known-bad JA3 hashes, is written but commented out until we record a lab hash.

7.1.6.2 Suricata Bridge

The bridge turns Suricata’s log into evidence. It polls the log once a second and remembers
both its position and the identity of the file, so it starts over if the log is rotated or truncated.
It reads only complete lines, which means a line Suricata is still writing is picked up on
the next poll. Blank and malformed lines are skipped, and only alert events are kept. Each
alert becomes Layer 1 evidence with the alert’s source address as the host. Strength follows
severity: 1.0 for a drop action or severity 1, 0.6 for severity 2, 0.3 for severity 3 and 0.25
otherwise. The signature weight is 3.0 by default. Alerts are not tied to a flow record, so
this evidence carries no flow id and is matched to hosts later.

7.1.6.3 ARP Monitor

The ARP monitor follows Algorithm 5.3. It looks only at ARP replies and gratuitous re-
quests, meaning requests whose sender and target address are the same. It raises evidence
of strength 1.0 in three cases: an address with a known MAC shows up with a different one;
a host claims the gateway address with a MAC other than the access point’s; or one MAC
sends more than ten replies in ten seconds. A second window across all MACs catches a
storm in which every packet uses a new random MAC.
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES  112

keeps the old ones and logs a warning, so a read failure cannot quietly switch detection off.
Memory is bounded because old entries are removed as they age. The gateway address and
the access point MAC are required settings, and the service will not start without them.

7.1.7 Layer 2: Anomaly Detection Service

7.1.7.1 Scoring and Calibration

The anomaly detector wraps a scikit-learn Isolation Forest. It first checks that each feature
vector has 78 values under the expected schema version, and it skips the flow if not. scikit-
learn returns the negative of the anomaly score in the original paper, so the result is negated
and clipped to the range 0 to 1 to match Equations 5.2 and 5.3. A flow produces anomaly
evidence only when its score is above the calibrated threshold.
The model is loaded from the database: the newest active package of the Isolation Forest
type, which also supplies the global threshold. If the load fails, the service logs a warning,
scores nothing and tries again every 60 seconds. The host-behaviour checks need no model,
so they keep working in the meantime.
The threshold is recalibrated as in Algorithm 5.4. With fewer than 2000 scores, the global
value is kept. Otherwise the 99.5th percentile of the local scores is used, limited to within
0.15 of the global threshold. Recalibration runs once seven days have passed. It leaves out
every flow from a host that has Layer 1 evidence or an active block, so an attacker present
during calibration cannot raise the threshold. From the same flows it also computes the
upload limit used by the exfiltration check, which is the 99.9th percentile of forward bytes
per flow segment. Taking this limit from the same sample is an addition to the algorithm as
designed.

7.1.7.2 Host Behaviour: Exfiltration and Beaconing

Exfiltration is checked per host, in 60-second buckets of outbound bytes. When a bucket
closes and the host already has a baseline, the bucket is flagged if its bytes exceed the
host’s moving average by four standard deviations and outbound traffic is at least three
times inbound. The bucket is then folded into the average with a smoothing factor of 0.3
(Equation 5.4). A host’s first bucket never raises evidence, because there is no baseline yet.
Flows are reported when they close, roll over or expire, so observations do not arrive in
time order. Closing a bucket as soon as a later one appears would split one window into
incomplete pieces. The tracker therefore keeps several open buckets per host and closes
one only when the newest end time seen across all hosts has passed it by the idle timeout
plus one bucket length. Older observations are counted and dropped. If no flow arrives for
the idle timeout plus two bucket lengths, all open buckets are flushed so the last window is
still checked.
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES  113

Beaconing is checked by grouping flows by host, destination (the server name if there is
one, otherwise the address) and JA3. Once a group has five connections, we compute the
coefficient of variation (Equation 5.5) for the gaps between connections and for the bytes
per connection. Evidence is raised only if both are at most 0.15 and the JA3 is rare, meaning
no more than two hosts have used it. Flows without a JA3 are never flagged. Requiring all
three conditions keeps regular but common traffic, such as browser update checks, from
being reported.

7.1.7.3 Service Loop

The service reads flow-ready messages through its own consumer group. For each one it
loads the flow record and passes it to the host-behaviour tracker and, if a model is loaded,
to the anomaly detector. Each piece of evidence gets its weight from the settings (anomaly
2.0, exfiltration 2.0, beaconing 2.5 by default) and is published. Between messages the
main loop retries a failed model load, checks whether recalibration is due, flushes quiet
exfiltration buckets and publishes a heartbeat. Each job has its own error handler, so a
failure in one cannot stop the heartbeat, which needs neither a model nor a calibration.

7.1.8 Self-Collected Dataset Tooling

7.1.8.1 Attack Scripts

Ten scripts generate the attacks for the self-collected dataset: port scan, SSH brute force,
SYN flood, DDoS, ARP spoofing, DNS spoofing, C2 beaconing, C2 with a chosen JA3,
data exfiltration and lateral movement. Seven wrap standard tools (nmap, hydra, hping3,
arpspoof and dnsspoof) and three are small Python TLS clients. All of them run through
one runner. It prints a reminder that attacks may only be run on an isolated lab network,
offers a dry-run mode, and records the run even if it is interrupted. For every run it appends
one JSON line with the attack, tool, attacker and victim addresses, UTC start and end time,
status and parameters. These records let us label every flow in a capture automatically
(Section 5.2.2).
Two scripts have limits worth stating. The DDoS script is a single-source flood, because we
have only one attacker machine, and each record says so. DNS spoofing works only while
ARP spoofing of the same victim is running, and the two are started separately.

7.1.8.2 Capture Sessions

A capture session is a directory named after its UTC start time and a label, with tcpdump
writing the pcap file. A benign session captures for a set time and then writes a manifest of
the Suricata alerts that fell inside it, so traffic Suricata already finds suspicious can be left
out before the capture is labelled benign (Section 5.2.2). An attack session starts tcpdump,
CHAPTER 7. IMPLEMENTATION AND TEST CASES                                          114

runs one attack with the session directory as its output folder, waits three seconds for the
last packets and stops the capture. Labelled flow tables are not produced here. That step
runs the flow extractor over each pcap and matches the flows to the attack records, and it
belongs to the offline training pipeline.

7.1.9 Components Under Development

The decision engine and the dashboard are partly built. The other components are designed
but not written.

    • The decision engine is partly built. It combines evidence into a decaying risk score
       for each host and decides whether to pass, alert or block (Algorithm 5.5). The rest of
       the logic and its link to the enforcer are still to do.

    • The dashboard is partly built. It shows the data already written to SQLite, and the
       remaining screens of Section 4.8 are still to do.

    • The Layer 3 classifier will use Random Forest or XGBoost on the same 78 features.
       Below a confidence threshold, a flow is labelled unknown.

    • The enforcer will turn block decisions into ipset entries, with an allowlist, a rate limit
       and exponential back-off (Algorithm 5.6).

    • The watchdog will read the heartbeats that every service already publishes and apply
       the configured failure mode.

    • The offline training pipeline will regenerate flows from the public datasets with the
       flow extractor and train the models.

7.1.10 Setup and Deployment

Running the system takes several manual steps. Python 3.11 or later, Redis and Suricata
have to be installed, and the Python dependencies go into a virtual environment from the
requirements file. Nothing detects the capture interface, so its name is found by hand. A
short Python call creates the database and seeds the default settings. Once the environ-
ment variables in Table 7.3 are exported, Redis and Suricata are started, followed by the
four services in separate terminals. The flow extractor and the ARP monitor need root or
CAP_NET_RAW. There is no combined launcher or systemd unit yet and the dashboard is
incomplete, so the most reliable ways to observe the system are the database and the JSON
logs.
